{"request_uuid": "caacdd4d-ad99-478a-ba94-72fe26930973", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T14:41:52.572494+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "patch", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "system", "content": "", "sections": {"preamble": "You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.", "tools": "<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- grep: Search file contents for patterns (respects .gitignore)\n- find: Find files by glob pattern (respects .gitignore)\n- ls: List directory contents\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>", "rules": "<rules>\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for precise changes (edits[].oldText must match exactly)\n- When changing multiple separate locations in one file, use one edit call with multiple entries in edits[] instead of multiple edit calls\n- Each edits[].oldText is matched against the original file, not after earlier edits are applied. Do not emit overlapping or nested edits. Merge nearby changes into one edit.\n- Keep edits[].oldText as small as possible while still being unique in the file. Do not pad with large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>", "docs": "<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>", "addendum": "<addendum>\nDataSQRL is a framework for building data pipelines and data products that ingest, process, store, and expose data as data lake/database views or through GraphQL, REST, or MCP APIs. It compiles SQL-like SQRL code into integrated data pipelines using Apache Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg, Vert.x server and other engines.\nDataSQRL runs and tests data pipelines locally or compiles deployment artifacts to deploy by the user or fully-managed by DataSQRL Cloud product offering.\n\n## Development Commands\n\nYou are running in **headless mode** inside a DataSQRL container using DataSQRL CLI version 0.11.x to:\n- compile a project with: `/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile -r <project root> <configs...> -b <sub-project>` \n- The `build/` folder contains the build artifacts for each sub-project seperately with `-b` flag and the build/<sub-project-name>/deploy/plan folder contains all deployment artifacts for each engine.\n\nYou are working on a git repository.\n\n**The compile command is slow and exclusive. Follow these rules without exception:**\n\n1. **Execute one command at a time.** Command invocations are synchronized on a shared lock. A second compile launched while one is running will queue and wait, it will NOT run in parallel. Never launch multiple compile from more than one place simultaneously.\n2. **Always run command synchronously in the foreground, and NEVER pipe it into anything.** No `run_in_background`, no `| tail`, `| grep`, `| head`, no `$(...)`. A run starts Postgres, Redpanda and a background helper that all inherit the command's stdout, and they can outlive the command (`head` also kills the build outright via SIGPIPE.) **Redirect to a file and read the file instead:** `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test x-test-package.json > /tmp/test.log 2>&1` then read `/tmp/test.log`.\n3. **Command can take multiple minutes.** Flink, Postgres, and Redpanda must start before tests begin. No output for the first minute or two is normal \u2014 do not assume failure.\n4. **Wait for `COMPILE_DONE: exit_code=N`.** This line marks the end of every compile. Only read results after seeing it. `exit_code=0` means success; any other value means failure.\n5. **If you see `COMPILE_QUEUED:`, stop.** Another compile is already running. Do not launch another. Wait for the current one to finish.\n\n## Writing Files\n\n**Write one file per writing call. Never assemble multiple files or an entire project in a single response.**\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}.sqrl                           # Main data processing logic\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-[dev/test/prod]-package.json   # Configuration per environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-connectors/                    # Project specific source/sink definitions, {name}- is optional\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 sources-[dev/test/prod].sqrl      # Source tables by environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/{name}/                     # Snapshot data for tests, subfolders for multiple projects\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-api/                           # API definitions (optional)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql             # Defines persisted queries, REST endpoints or MCP tools\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                            # Test queries (.graphql)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh                          # Runs the test suites \u2014 the agent, CI and you all run this one script\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md                             # Project documentation\n```\n\nLayouts vary \u2014 treat the above as the common shape, not a required one.\n\nYour project may be alone in its repository, or it may sit alongside other SQRL projects and shared\nmodules. Those are **siblings \u2014 outside your project, not part of it**. Their directory names are\nwhatever the repository author chose, so never assume a particular name: read\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` for what is actually present. For example:\n\n```\n{repo root}/                   # mounted READ-ONLY\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {your-project}/            # the ONLY directory you can write to\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {another-project}/         # e.g. a second pipeline \u2014 reference only\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 {shared-module}/           # e.g. a data catalog or metrics layer \u2014 reference only\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThe `*-package.json` files configure every aspect of a DataSQRL project (main script, engines, API, tests, compiler, deployment). \nInvoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill before editing any configuration file (base config and environment overlays, `script.include` for included modules like data catalog). \nEach (sub-)project, and each environment of those (sub-)projects has its own configuration. \nWhen you create a pipeline project or sub-project, give it a `-test`, a `-dev` and a `-prod` environment overlay. Leave out `-dev` or `-prod` only when the requirements say that the environment is not needed. The `-dev` overlay uses sources and sinks that run on a local machine.\nTo prepare tests, compile and run tests; first invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill and then apply the steps.\nAlways check the `package.json` files for authoritative file paths, and align `README.md` with the project-specific settings.\n\n## Skill Usage Policy\n\nBefore any file change, work through this checklist:\n\n1. State what you are about to do (file type, what it changes).\n2. Check each skill's trigger condition against your action and invoke every match:\n\n| Trigger condition                                                                                                     | Skill                      | Required?                                          |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n                                                                                                          | Writing or editing a `.sqrl` file                                                                                     | `/implement-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing any source/sink definition file (typically a `connectors/` folder, or a catalog you own like `data_catalog/`) | `/manage-connector`        | **MUST** \u2014 even when following an existing pattern |\n| Editing a `*-package.json`                                                                                            | `/configure-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` API schema                                 | `/design-api`              | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| When requirements specify the API schema that the SQRL project requires                                               | `/from-api`                | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| Writing a `/*+test*/` table, modifying snapshots, creating or editing `run-tests.sh`, or first test run on a project  | `/test-sqrl`               | **MUST** \u2014 no exceptions                           |\n| Creating or editing data catalog                                                                                      | `/build-catalog`           | **MUST** - no excpetions                           |\n| Test or compile error not immediately obvious                                                                         | `/troubleshoot-test`       | If applicable                                      |\n| Given provided source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling                                               | `/data-observation`        | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing or updating `README.md` or any other documentation                                                            | `/write-documentation`     | If applicable                                      |\n| A large data file is present, or a run reports `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`                                                   | `/handle-large-data-files` | If applicable \u2014 reactive                     |\n\n3. For non-MUST skills: only skip if you explicitly state why it does not apply to this specific action.\n4. For **MUST** skills: \"I already know the pattern\" is not a valid reason to skip.\n5. **Skill form takes precedence over plan-step wording.** Where a plan step and a skill differ on import form, connector pattern, API exposure, hint placement, or syntax, implement the skill's form, then correct the step's text to describe what you built. Plan steps state intent and are written without a compiler.\n\nInvoke skills immediately before the file change they apply to \u2014 not all at once at session start.\n\nConsult skills before reading templates \u2014 skills contain the essential patterns; templates are for structural reference only.\n\n\n## Other Projects in the Repository \u2014 Read-Only\n\nYour project can sit inside a repository that also holds other SQRL projects and shared modules\n(a data catalog is the common case, but there may be several, with any names). The whole repository\nis mounted **READ-ONLY**; only your own project directory is writable.\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists what is actually there \u2014 rely on it rather than on assumed names\nor layouts.\n\n- **Never** create, edit, copy or delete anything outside your project.\n- **Never copy** a sibling's files into your project. If you need what a shared module provides,\n  declare it under `script.include` and import it by namespace.\n- **Never read another project's build folders** (`build/`, `build-*/`) \u2014 they are transient compiler output. The `.sqrl`, `package.json`, and `README.md` files in the project folder contain all relevant information\n- Only include modules that this project **actually needs**. \n- Sibling projects are useful as **worked examples**: how they configure `script.include`, how they\n  import a module, how they lay out connectors and tests. Follow those patterns where they fit.\n\n## Scope \u2014 Do Not Change What You Were Not Asked to Change\n\nA patch run changes only what the request asks for, plus whatever that change breaks.\n\n- Touch a file only if the request names it, or it must change for the request to compile or pass its tests.\n- Update the project documentation according to changes.\n- **\"For consistency with the new code\" is never a reason to rewrite existing code.** Match the\n  existing code's conventions in what you add; never migrate the existing code to match yours unless explicitly asked.\n\n## Data Processing\n\nThe data ingestion, processing, and serving logic is implemented in the main script using a SQL dialect called SQRL which is an extension of Flink SQL 2.3. The main script may import additional SQRL scripts: local ones by relative path, scripts of another SQRL project (e.g. a shared data catalog) by the namespace declared under configuration file: invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Included Projects) and the `/implement-sqrl` skill (SQRL Language Spec \u2192 IMPORT Statement). SQRL scripts can import Flink functions implemented in Java for custom processing logic that cannot be expressed in SQL: invoke the `/implement-udf` skill.\n\nAll data processing is implemented in SQRL scripts and imported functions.\n\n## Sources and Sinks\n\nSource/sink tables are defined with `CREATE TABLE` statements - often in dedicated SQRL files separated by environment in a connectors folder and imported/exported from the main script.\n\nSources are used to ingest data for processing. Sinks are used to export data to external systems. `CREATE TABLE` statements with a preceding `/*+engine(..) */` hint are sources/sinks that are managed by DataSQRL with the external resources (e.g. Kafka topic or Iceberg table) created and managed automatically.\n\nSources and sinks local to the project (usually in a `connectors` folder) can be updated when the request requires it \u2014 see **Scope** above. Sources, sinks and imported view definitions that come from a shared module (a data catalog or similar, whatever it is named) belong to other teams/projects and cannot be changed \u2014 they are mounted read-only. Always check whether an import is local or comes from a shared module before making changes.\n\n## API\n\nDataSQRL uses GraphQL as the authoritative model for the data serving API which exposes the processed data if the server engine is enabled. The GraphQL API maps tables in the main script (and those imported inline) to queries/mutations/subscriptions plus associated types and relationships to fields. SQRL supports table functions to define custom endpoints.\nThe GraphQL schema is either inferred from SQRL or defined explicitly and configured in the package.json. The inferred GraphQL schema is written to `<build-folder>/inferred_schema.graphqls` after compilation (the folder named by `-b`, else `build/`).\n\nREST and MCP (model context protocol) endpoints are created automatically for each GraphQL query and mutation or defined explicitly as GraphQL operations.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe test command executes the GraphQL queries in the configured test folder and the table queries marked with the `/*+test*/` hint that the test server's schema exposes, and stores the results in the snapshots folder. Tests pass when snapshots are identical or no records are returned on a test marked `no_rows`. `${DATA_PATH}` is set by the framework and does not need to be configured. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for writing tests and test data, and for running the test packages.\n\n## Documentation\n\nClearly and concisely document any code you implement focusing on implementation choices and intent. Do not document what is implemented but focus on the *why?*.\n\n# Task context (persistent \u2014 keep these in mind across compaction)\n\n## DataSQRL skills\n\nThe DataSQRL skills available. Read the ones your work touches before you\nwrite or change anything, and open the reference pages they point to.\n\n### /build-catalog \u2014 Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.\n### /configure-sqrl \u2014 Use when configuring a DataSQRL project's `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.\n### /data-observation \u2014 Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.\n### /design-api \u2014 Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.\n### /from-api \u2014 Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).\n### /handle-large-data-files \u2014 Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.\n### /implement-sqrl \u2014 Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.\n### /implement-udf \u2014 Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.\n### /implement-ui \u2014 Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.\n### /manage-connector \u2014 Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.\n### /monitor-pipeline \u2014 Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline's monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.\n### /optimize-deployment \u2014 Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.<flink|postgres|vertx>.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.\n### /test-sqrl \u2014 Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.\n### /troubleshoot-test \u2014 Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.\n### /write-documentation \u2014 Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```\n\n## Repository inventory\nThis repo contains other SQRL projects/modules. `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists them (paths, READMEs, SQRL files, package summaries) \u2014 read it before deciding how to build. They are mounted READ-ONLY: never edit them, never copy their files into this project (include shared modules via `script.include` in `*-package.json` files instead), and never read their `build/` directories.\n\n## Requirements\nYour requirements are in `adr/requirements_20261124-144152.md` (authoritative). Read it before implementing and re-read it after any compaction. There is no implementation plan or checklist for this run \u2014 implement the requirements directly.\n</addendum>", "project_context": "<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/workspace/loan-payments/AGENTS.md\">\nDataSQRL is a framework for building data pipelines and data products that ingest, process, store, and expose data as data lake/database views or through GraphQL, REST, or MCP APIs. It compiles SQL-like SQRL code into integrated data pipelines using Apache Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg, Vert.x server and other engines.\nDataSQRL runs and tests data pipelines locally or compiles deployment artifacts to deploy by the user or fully-managed by DataSQRL Cloud product offering.\n\n## Development Commands\n\nYou are running in **headless mode** inside a DataSQRL container using DataSQRL CLI version 0.11.x to:\n- compile a project with: `/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile -r <project root> <configs...> -b <sub-project>` \n- The `build/` folder contains the build artifacts for each sub-project seperately with `-b` flag and the build/<sub-project-name>/deploy/plan folder contains all deployment artifacts for each engine.\n\nYou are working on a git repository.\n\n**The compile command is slow and exclusive. Follow these rules without exception:**\n\n1. **Execute one command at a time.** Command invocations are synchronized on a shared lock. A second compile launched while one is running will queue and wait, it will NOT run in parallel. Never launch multiple compile from more than one place simultaneously.\n2. **Always run command synchronously in the foreground, and NEVER pipe it into anything.** No `run_in_background`, no `| tail`, `| grep`, `| head`, no `$(...)`. A run starts Postgres, Redpanda and a background helper that all inherit the command's stdout, and they can outlive the command (`head` also kills the build outright via SIGPIPE.) **Redirect to a file and read the file instead:** `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test x-test-package.json > /tmp/test.log 2>&1` then read `/tmp/test.log`.\n3. **Command can take multiple minutes.** Flink, Postgres, and Redpanda must start before tests begin. No output for the first minute or two is normal \u2014 do not assume failure.\n4. **Wait for `COMPILE_DONE: exit_code=N`.** This line marks the end of every compile. Only read results after seeing it. `exit_code=0` means success; any other value means failure.\n5. **If you see `COMPILE_QUEUED:`, stop.** Another compile is already running. Do not launch another. Wait for the current one to finish.\n\n## Writing Files\n\n**Write one file per writing call. Never assemble multiple files or an entire project in a single response.**\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}.sqrl                           # Main data processing logic\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-[dev/test/prod]-package.json   # Configuration per environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-connectors/                    # Project specific source/sink definitions, {name}- is optional\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 sources-[dev/test/prod].sqrl      # Source tables by environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/{name}/                     # Snapshot data for tests, subfolders for multiple projects\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-api/                           # API definitions (optional)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql             # Defines persisted queries, REST endpoints or MCP tools\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                            # Test queries (.graphql)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh                          # Runs the test suites \u2014 the agent, CI and you all run this one script\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md                             # Project documentation\n```\n\nLayouts vary \u2014 treat the above as the common shape, not a required one.\n\nYour project may be alone in its repository, or it may sit alongside other SQRL projects and shared\nmodules. Those are **siblings \u2014 outside your project, not part of it**. Their directory names are\nwhatever the repository author chose, so never assume a particular name: read\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` for what is actually present. For example:\n\n```\n{repo root}/                   # mounted READ-ONLY\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {your-project}/            # the ONLY directory you can write to\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {another-project}/         # e.g. a second pipeline \u2014 reference only\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 {shared-module}/           # e.g. a data catalog or metrics layer \u2014 reference only\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThe `*-package.json` files configure every aspect of a DataSQRL project (main script, engines, API, tests, compiler, deployment). \nInvoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill before editing any configuration file (base config and environment overlays, `script.include` for included modules like data catalog). \nEach (sub-)project, and each environment of those (sub-)projects has its own configuration. \nWhen you create a pipeline project or sub-project, give it a `-test`, a `-dev` and a `-prod` environment overlay. Leave out `-dev` or `-prod` only when the requirements say that the environment is not needed. The `-dev` overlay uses sources and sinks that run on a local machine.\nTo prepare tests, compile and run tests; first invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill and then apply the steps.\nAlways check the `package.json` files for authoritative file paths, and align `README.md` with the project-specific settings.\n\n## Skill Usage Policy\n\nBefore any file change, work through this checklist:\n\n1. State what you are about to do (file type, what it changes).\n2. Check each skill's trigger condition against your action and invoke every match:\n\n| Trigger condition                                                                                                     | Skill                      | Required?                                          |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n                                                                                                          | Writing or editing a `.sqrl` file                                                                                     | `/implement-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing any source/sink definition file (typically a `connectors/` folder, or a catalog you own like `data_catalog/`) | `/manage-connector`        | **MUST** \u2014 even when following an existing pattern |\n| Editing a `*-package.json`                                                                                            | `/configure-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` API schema                                 | `/design-api`              | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| When requirements specify the API schema that the SQRL project requires                                               | `/from-api`                | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| Writing a `/*+test*/` table, modifying snapshots, creating or editing `run-tests.sh`, or first test run on a project  | `/test-sqrl`               | **MUST** \u2014 no exceptions                           |\n| Creating or editing data catalog                                                                                      | `/build-catalog`           | **MUST** - no excpetions                           |\n| Test or compile error not immediately obvious                                                                         | `/troubleshoot-test`       | If applicable                                      |\n| Given provided source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling                                               | `/data-observation`        | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing or updating `README.md` or any other documentation                                                            | `/write-documentation`     | If applicable                                      |\n| A large data file is present, or a run reports `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`                                                   | `/handle-large-data-files` | If applicable \u2014 reactive                     |\n\n3. For non-MUST skills: only skip if you explicitly state why it does not apply to this specific action.\n4. For **MUST** skills: \"I already know the pattern\" is not a valid reason to skip.\n5. **Skill form takes precedence over plan-step wording.** Where a plan step and a skill differ on import form, connector pattern, API exposure, hint placement, or syntax, implement the skill's form, then correct the step's text to describe what you built. Plan steps state intent and are written without a compiler.\n\nInvoke skills immediately before the file change they apply to \u2014 not all at once at session start.\n\nConsult skills before reading templates \u2014 skills contain the essential patterns; templates are for structural reference only.\n\n\n## Other Projects in the Repository \u2014 Read-Only\n\nYour project can sit inside a repository that also holds other SQRL projects and shared modules\n(a data catalog is the common case, but there may be several, with any names). The whole repository\nis mounted **READ-ONLY**; only your own project directory is writable.\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists what is actually there \u2014 rely on it rather than on assumed names\nor layouts.\n\n- **Never** create, edit, copy or delete anything outside your project.\n- **Never copy** a sibling's files into your project. If you need what a shared module provides,\n  declare it under `script.include` and import it by namespace.\n- **Never read another project's build folders** (`build/`, `build-*/`) \u2014 they are transient compiler output. The `.sqrl`, `package.json`, and `README.md` files in the project folder contain all relevant information\n- Only include modules that this project **actually needs**. \n- Sibling projects are useful as **worked examples**: how they configure `script.include`, how they\n  import a module, how they lay out connectors and tests. Follow those patterns where they fit.\n\n## Scope \u2014 Do Not Change What You Were Not Asked to Change\n\nA patch run changes only what the request asks for, plus whatever that change breaks.\n\n- Touch a file only if the request names it, or it must change for the request to compile or pass its tests.\n- Update the project documentation according to changes.\n- **\"For consistency with the new code\" is never a reason to rewrite existing code.** Match the\n  existing code's conventions in what you add; never migrate the existing code to match yours unless explicitly asked.\n\n## Data Processing\n\nThe data ingestion, processing, and serving logic is implemented in the main script using a SQL dialect called SQRL which is an extension of Flink SQL 2.3. The main script may import additional SQRL scripts: local ones by relative path, scripts of another SQRL project (e.g. a shared data catalog) by the namespace declared under configuration file: invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Included Projects) and the `/implement-sqrl` skill (SQRL Language Spec \u2192 IMPORT Statement). SQRL scripts can import Flink functions implemented in Java for custom processing logic that cannot be expressed in SQL: invoke the `/implement-udf` skill.\n\nAll data processing is implemented in SQRL scripts and imported functions.\n\n## Sources and Sinks\n\nSource/sink tables are defined with `CREATE TABLE` statements - often in dedicated SQRL files separated by environment in a connectors folder and imported/exported from the main script.\n\nSources are used to ingest data for processing. Sinks are used to export data to external systems. `CREATE TABLE` statements with a preceding `/*+engine(..) */` hint are sources/sinks that are managed by DataSQRL with the external resources (e.g. Kafka topic or Iceberg table) created and managed automatically.\n\nSources and sinks local to the project (usually in a `connectors` folder) can be updated when the request requires it \u2014 see **Scope** above. Sources, sinks and imported view definitions that come from a shared module (a data catalog or similar, whatever it is named) belong to other teams/projects and cannot be changed \u2014 they are mounted read-only. Always check whether an import is local or comes from a shared module before making changes.\n\n## API\n\nDataSQRL uses GraphQL as the authoritative model for the data serving API which exposes the processed data if the server engine is enabled. The GraphQL API maps tables in the main script (and those imported inline) to queries/mutations/subscriptions plus associated types and relationships to fields. SQRL supports table functions to define custom endpoints.\nThe GraphQL schema is either inferred from SQRL or defined explicitly and configured in the package.json. The inferred GraphQL schema is written to `<build-folder>/inferred_schema.graphqls` after compilation (the folder named by `-b`, else `build/`).\n\nREST and MCP (model context protocol) endpoints are created automatically for each GraphQL query and mutation or defined explicitly as GraphQL operations.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe test command executes the GraphQL queries in the configured test folder and the table queries marked with the `/*+test*/` hint that the test server's schema exposes, and stores the results in the snapshots folder. Tests pass when snapshots are identical or no records are returned on a test marked `no_rows`. `${DATA_PATH}` is set by the framework and does not need to be configured. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for writing tests and test data, and for running the test packages.\n\n## Documentation\n\nClearly and concisely document any code you implement focusing on implementation choices and intent. Do not document what is implemented but focus on the *why?*.\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>", "skills": "<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nWhen a skill file references a relative path, resolve it against the skill directory (parent of SKILL.md / dirname of the path) and use that absolute path in tool commands.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>build-catalog</name>\n    <description>Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/build-catalog/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>configure-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when configuring a DataSQRL project&apos;s `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>data-observation</name>\n    <description>Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/data-observation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>design-api</name>\n    <description>Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/design-api/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>from-api</name>\n    <description>Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/from-api/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>handle-large-data-files</name>\n    <description>Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/handle-large-data-files/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-udf</name>\n    <description>Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-udf/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-ui</name>\n    <description>Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-ui/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>manage-connector</name>\n    <description>Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/manage-connector/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>monitor-pipeline</name>\n    <description>Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline&apos;s monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/monitor-pipeline/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>optimize-deployment</name>\n    <description>Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.&lt;flink|postgres|vertx&gt;.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/optimize-deployment/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/test-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>troubleshoot-test</name>\n    <description>Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/troubleshoot-test/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>write-documentation</name>\n    <description>Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/write-documentation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>", "cwd": "<cwd>\n/workspace/loan-payments\n</cwd>"}, "timestamp": 1795531312569, "toolsAdded": [{"name": "read", "description": "Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"}, "offset": {"type": "number", "description": "Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of lines to read"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "bash", "description": "Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["command"], "properties": {"command": {"type": "string", "description": "Shell command to execute"}, "timeout": {"type": "number", "description": "Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "edit", "description": "Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path", "edits"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"}, "edits": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["oldText", "newText"], "properties": {"oldText": {"type": "string", "description": "Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."}, "newText": {"type": "string", "description": "Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}}, "description": "One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "write", "description": "Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path", "content"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"}, "content": {"type": "string", "description": "Content to write to the file"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "grep", "description": "Search file contents for a pattern. Returns matching lines with file paths and line numbers. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 100 matches or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Long lines are truncated to 500 chars.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["pattern"], "properties": {"pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "Search pattern (regex or literal string)"}, "path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory or file to search (default: current directory)"}, "glob": {"type": "string", "description": "Filter files by glob pattern, e.g. '*.ts' or '**/*.spec.ts'"}, "ignoreCase": {"type": "boolean", "description": "Case-insensitive search (default: false)"}, "literal": {"type": "boolean", "description": "Treat pattern as literal string instead of regex (default: false)"}, "context": {"type": "number", "description": "Number of lines to show before and after each match (default: 0)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of matches to return (default: 100)"}}}}, {"name": "find", "description": "Search for files by glob pattern. Returns matching file paths relative to the search directory. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 1000 results or 50KB (whichever is hit first).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["pattern"], "properties": {"pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "Glob pattern to match files, e.g. '*.ts', '**/*.json', or 'src/**/*.spec.ts'"}, "path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory to search in (default: current directory)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of results (default: 1000)"}}}}, {"name": "ls", "description": "List directory contents. Returns entries sorted alphabetically, with '/' suffix for directories. Includes dotfiles. Output is truncated to 500 entries or 50KB (whichever is hit first).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory to list (default: current directory)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of entries to return (default: 500)"}}}}]}}
{"request_uuid": "caacdd4d-ad99-478a-ba94-72fe26930973", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T14:41:52.573920+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "patch", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# DataSQRL Patch Task\n\n## Context\n\nYou are making a **small, well-scoped change** to an existing DataSQRL project.\nThe request below is the whole requirement.\n\n## User requirements\n\nYour primary goal is to implement the following user requirements:\n---\ntype: user-requirements\nmode: patch\ndomains:\n  - finance\nscenario: pipeline\n---\n\nAdd a dev environment overlay `loan-payments-dev-package.json` for local development, layered on top of `loan-payments-shared-package.json`. Use exactly this content (copied from the reference file finance-demo/loan-payments/loan-payments-dev-package.json in another checkout):\n{\n  \"script\": { \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"loan-payments-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"loan-payments-api\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"bG9hbi1wYXltZW50cy10ZXN0LXNlY3JldC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\nIt reads the catalog test data (environment \"test\") and uses a fixed dev JWT issuer, audience and HS256 key. Declare `dev` as an environment in run-tests.sh so `./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev` compiles the loan-payments sub-project with loan-payments-shared-package.json + loan-payments-dev-package.json. Document the dev package and how to run it locally in README.md.\n\n\n---\n\n## 1. Understand the impact before editing\n\nRead the project first and establish what the change affects. Work through these and state which apply and which do not:\n\n- **Logic** \u2014 tables, relationships, and computations.\n- **API surface** \u2014 the GraphQL schema and operations (`*.graphqls`, `*.graphql`), including\n  argument and return types.\n- **Configuration** \u2014 `*-package.json` engine, connector, or environment settings.\n- **UI behavior** \u2014 infer UI work from required interactions, even without an explicit UI request.\n- **Documentation** \u2014 `README.md` and any docs describing the system and its components.\n- **Tests**\n\n\nBefore any file change, always check each skill and understand what they are used for. Match your action against the triggers below and invoke every skill that\nmatches, read the skill first, then make the change it covers.\n\n- Invoke the `/implement-sqrl` skill when writing or editing any `.sqrl` file.\n- Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill when writing any file in `connectors/` or `data_catalog/`.\n- Invoke the `/design-api` skill when adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE statement, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` file.\n- Invoke the `/from-api` skill when the requirements themselves specify the API to expose (GraphQL schema, REST/OpenAPI endpoints, MCP tools).\n- Invoke `/implement-ui` when adding or changing screens or interactions.\n- Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill when writing or editing `/*+test*/` tables, modifying snapshots, or running tests.\n- Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill when writing or editing a `*-package.json`.\n- Invoke the `/troubleshoot-test` skill when a test fails and the cause is not immediately obvious.\n- Invoke the `/write-documentation` skill when updating `README.md` or other docs.\n- Invoke the `/data-observation` skill when inspecting provided source/sample/test data files.\n- Invoke the `/handle-large-data-files` skill when a large data file is present, or a run reported `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`.\n\n## 2. Focused Change\n\nOnly edit files named in the request or needed to implement it, including any implied UI work. Also update files needed to compile and test the changes, and documentation that describes the changed behavior.\n\n**If the request cannot be done as a small change and requires larger implementation**, say so plainly in your final message, explaining what makes it larger than a patch. The user may need to re-run it through complete workflow (first planning and then implementation)\n\nIf the change makes a test's expectation wrong, update it. Never add,\nweaken, or delete a test to get a green run.\n\nIf the change makes documentation wrong, update it. Invoke the `/write-documentation` skill to correct documentation.\n\n## 3. Compile and test\n\n**Compile** every affected (sub-)project with the help of the `/configure-sqrl` skill. Then **test** through the project's test runner script, `run-tests.sh`. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for test details. Scope it to the affected sub-projects while iterating.\n\n## 4. Review: \nAnalyze compiler output and test results\n\n## 5. Iterate: \nIf there are errors or test failures, fix them and repeat \n\n* Treat every test failure as evidence: A failing test is telling you something about the change. \n\n## Response\n\nYour last action must be a successful run of `./run-tests.sh`, scoped with the names of every affected sub-projects. Output that exact invocation as your final line, no markdown and no explanation:\n\n```bash\n./run-tests.sh [sub-project-name]   # only the affected sub-project(s)\n./run-tests.sh                      # everything\n```\n\n\n## Other projects in this repository (read-only)\n\nThis repository contains 3 other SQRL director(ies) besides your project. They are mounted **READ-ONLY**: you can read them for reference, but you cannot and must not modify them, and you must never copy their files into your project.\n\nA full inventory \u2014 paths, READMEs, SQRL file lists and package summaries \u2014 is in `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md`. **Read it before planning or implementing.**\n\n**Shared module(s) \u2014 investigate these FIRST:**\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 declared under script.include by customer\n\n**Other project(s) \u2014 read after the shared module(s):**\n- `customer` (project)\n- `lending` (project)\n\nFor each: read its `README.md` first if it has one, then its `.sqrl` files, then its `package.json`. **Never read their `build/` directories** \u2014 that is compiler output and is deliberately excluded from the inventory.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```"}], "timestamp": 1795531312569}}
