{"request_uuid": "caacdd4d-ad99-478a-ba94-72fe26930973", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T14:41:55.606721+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "patch", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "tool_execution_update", "toolCallId": "call_01a20e01989e76138e90e2e5", "toolName": "bash", "args": {"command": "cd /workspace/loan-payments && echo \"=== run-tests.sh ===\" && cat run-tests.sh && echo \"=== shared package ===\" && cat loan-payments-shared-package.json && echo \"=== prod package ===\" && cat loan-payments-prod-package.json && echo \"=== api test package ===\" && cat loan-payments-api-test-package.json && echo \"=== table test package ===\" && cat loan-payments-table-test-package.json && echo \"=== README ===\" && cat README.md"}, "partialResult": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "=== run-tests.sh ===\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# This project's suites \u2014 one command, deterministic exit code.\n# Used by the DataSQRL agent and by CI.\n#\n# USAGE\n#   ./run-tests.sh [options] [<sub-project> ...]\n#\n#   <sub-project>       zero or more names, in any order, selecting which suites run.\n#                       Give none to run them all. Options may appear before, after or\n#                       between the names.\n#\n#   --compile           compile instead of running tests\n#   --test              run tests (the default)\n#   --env <name>        which environment's configs to use (default: test).\n#                       `--env=<name>` is accepted too.\n#   --list-envs         print the environments this project declares, one line\n#   --list-subprojects  print the sub-projects this project ships, one line\n#   --list-invocations  print what would run and run nothing, one\n#                       `SQRL_INVOCATION <sub-project> <verb> -r <root> <config...> -b <name>`\n#                       line per suite \u2014 exactly the arguments a real run would use\n#\n# EXAMPLES (using this project's own sub-project, event_audit)\n#   ./run-tests.sh                                     test every suite, env test\n#   ./run-tests.sh event_audit                         test just that sub-project\n#   ./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev                 compile every suite as dev\n#   ./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod event_audit    compile just that one, as prod\n#   ./run-tests.sh --list-invocations --compile --env dev     show what would run\n#\n#   A project with several sub-projects names as many as it likes:\n#   ./run-tests.sh <sub-project> <sub-project>\n#\n# An unknown sub-project name, or an environment this project does not declare, is an error\n# (exit 2) \u2014 never a silent no-op that runs nothing and reports success.\n#\n# WRITING THIS FILE FOR A PROJECT\n#   Three blocks below are marked \"EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT\": the ENVS/SUBPROJECTS declarations,\n#   the `suite` table, and the optional setup/teardown hooks. Everything else is boilerplate \u2014\n#   copy it verbatim.\n#\n#   The `suite` table is the ONE place a project's config layering is written down: for each\n#   sub-project and each environment, the exact configs to layer, in merge order.\n#\nset -uo pipefail\n# `set -e` (exit on the first error) is not used to allow for more granular error handling.\n\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\"\n\n# Where this project sits relative to the compiler's mount root. Inside the DataSQRL agent the mount\n# root is the repository, so a project in a subdirectory is reached with the prefix the\n# orchestrator exports; empty everywhere else, meaning the project IS the mount root.\n#\n# It is passed as `-r <root>` rather than glued onto every config path. Both forms are equivalent\n# to the compiler, but `-r` is REQUIRED once `-b` points inside build/: without it the compiler\n# resolves script.main against the wrong root and fails with \"Could not find main SQRL script\".\nP=\"${SQRL_PACKAGE_PREFIX:-}\"\nif [ -n \"$P\" ]; then\n  ROOT=\"${P%/}\"                                   # the DataSQRL agent told us where we are\nelif ROOT=\"$(git rev-parse --show-prefix 2>/dev/null)\"; then\n  ROOT=\"${ROOT%/}\"; ROOT=\"${ROOT:-.}\"             # a repo checkout: our path within it\nelse\n  ROOT=\".\"                                        # not in a repo: this project is the root\nfi\n\n# The DataSQRL CLI version the DataSQRL agent container ships; CI and local runs default to the same\n# one so snapshots match. Override with SQRL_VERSION.\nSQRL_VERSION=\"${SQRL_VERSION:-0.12.5}\"\n# Fallback tag for when that version is not published yet (a release in flight). Resolved once,\n# on the first docker invocation, and never inside the DataSQRL agent \u2014 the container has its own CLI.\nSQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE=\"${SQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE:-datasqrl/cmd:dev}\"\nIMAGE=\"\"\n\nresolve_image() {\n  [ -n \"$IMAGE\" ] && return 0\n  local want=\"datasqrl/cmd:${SQRL_VERSION}\"\n  if docker image inspect \"$want\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || docker pull -q \"$want\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    IMAGE=\"$want\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n  IMAGE=\"$SQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE\"\n  # Worth a warning, not a failure: snapshots were generated with the pinned version, so a\n  # different CLI can legitimately produce different output and fail tests that are fine.\n  printf 'warning: datasqrl/cmd:%s is not available; falling back to %s\\n' \\\n    \"$SQRL_VERSION\" \"$IMAGE\" >&2\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# The environments this project's configs cover, and the sub-projects it ships. Both are\n# declared rather than discovered, so that asking for something this project does not have\n# fails loudly instead of matching nothing, running zero suites and still exiting 0.\nENVS=\"test prod\"\nSUBPROJECTS=\"loan-payments loan-payments_table_test\"\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nVERB=test\nENV=test\nLIST=\"\"\nSELECTED=\"\"\nwhile [ $# -gt 0 ]; do\n  case \"$1\" in\n    --compile)   VERB=compile ;;\n    --test)      VERB=test ;;\n    --env)       ENV=\"${2:-}\"; shift ;;\n    --env=*)     ENV=\"${1#--env=}\" ;;\n    --list-invocations) LIST=1 ;;\n    --list-envs) echo \"$ENVS\"; exit 0 ;;\n    --list-subprojects) echo \"$SUBPROJECTS\"; exit 0 ;;\n    -*)          echo \"unknown option: $1\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n    *)           SELECTED=\"$SELECTED $1\" ;;\n  esac\n  shift\ndone\ncase \" $ENVS \" in\n  *\" $ENV \"*) ;;\n  *) echo \"unknown env '$ENV' \u2014 this project declares: $ENVS\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\nesac\n\nfor s in $SELECTED; do\n  case \" $SUBPROJECTS \" in\n    *\" $s \"*) ;;\n    *) echo \"unknown sub-project '$s' \u2014 this project ships: $SUBPROJECTS\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n  esac\ndone\n\nselected() {\n  [ -z \"$SELECTED\" ] && return 0\n  case \" $SELECTED \" in *\" $1 \"*) return 0 ;; esac\n  return 1\n}\n\n# Each suite writes its own build folder (the -b flag in the driver loop below), so one suite's\n# artifacts never overwrite a sibling's. `-b <name>` names a subfolder of build/: DataSQRL\n# resolves `-b fraud_store` to <project-root>/build/fraud_store\n\nsqrl() {\n  local verb=\"$1\"; shift\n  # Every named config must exist. A missing one means the suite line and this environment\n  # disagree, and compiling a partial argument list would quietly review the wrong thing.\n  local a missing=0\n  for a in \"$@\"; do\n    case \"$a\" in\n      *.json) [ -f \"$a\" ] || { echo \"missing config: $a\" >&2; missing=1; } ;;\n    esac\n  done\n  [ \"$missing\" = 1 ] && return 1\n  # List mode: report what would run and return. Bash has already expanded the environment and\n  # the path prefix, so the caller reads the real argument list without parsing this file. The\n  # sub-project is named first so each line stands on its own.\n  if [ -n \"$LIST\" ]; then\n    printf 'SQRL_INVOCATION %s %s -r %s %s\\n' \"$SUITE\" \"$verb\" \"$ROOT\" \"$*\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n\n  if [ -x /opt/agent/cmd.sh ]; then\n    /opt/agent/cmd.sh \"$verb\" -r \"$ROOT\" \"$@\"     # inside the DataSQRL agent container\n    return\n  fi\n  # CI / local developer machine: run the DataSQRL CLI image. Mount the REPOSITORY root at\n  # /workspace (not just this project) so a sibling shared module this project consumes exists\n  # inside the container too; `-r <project>` then points the compiler at this project.\n  local root\n  root=\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)\" || root=\"$PWD\"\n  # The image runs in UTC, and every template's snapshots are generated in UTC. The container's\n  # default is left alone. TZ passes through only when set explicitly.\n  resolve_image\n  docker run --rm ${TZ:+-e TZ=\"$TZ\"} -v \"$root\":/workspace \\\n    \"$IMAGE\" \"$verb\" -r \"$ROOT\" \"$@\"\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT (optional): setup \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# The test sources are the read-only `../data-catalog` submodule's\n# lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/*.jsonl fixtures. The DataSQRL test runner sets ${DATA_PATH}\n# to that testdata folder. On a clean checkout initialize the submodule; in the agent container it\n# is already mounted, so the guard skips the fetch.\nif [ -z \"$LIST\" ] && [ ! -d ../data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata ]; then\n  git submodule update --init --recursive || exit 1\nfi\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT: the suite table \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# One line per (sub-project, environment), naming that combination's configs in merge order \u2014\n# base first, environment config last, since a later file overrides an earlier one. Write the\n# filenames out literally; any names work. Cover every pair of SUBPROJECTS x ENVS that this\n# project actually has, and leave out the ones it does not: asking for a pair that is missing\n# reports an error rather than compiling something half-declared.\n#\n#   <sub-project>/<env>) echo \"<base config> <env config>\" ;;\n#\nsuite() {\n  case \"$1/$2\" in\n    loan-payments/test)             echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json\" ;;\n    loan-payments/prod)             echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-prod-package.json\" ;;\n    loan-payments_table_test/test)  echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json\" ;;\n    loan-payments_table_test/prod)  echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json\" ;;\n    *) return 1 ;;\n  esac\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# Every selected sub-project runs even after one fails, so a single run reports every failure.\nfail=0\nSUITE=\"\"\nfor sub in $SUBPROJECTS; do\n  selected \"$sub\" || continue\n  SUITE=\"$sub\"\n  args=\"$(suite \"$sub\" \"$ENV\")\" || {\n    echo \"no '$ENV' configs declared for sub-project '$sub'\" >&2\n    fail=1\n    continue\n  }\n  # shellcheck disable=SC2086  # the declaration is a space-separated argument list, on purpose\n  sqrl \"$VERB\" $args -b \"$sub\" || fail=1\ndone\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT (optional): teardown \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# [ -n \"$LIST\" ] || ./scripts/teardown.sh\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nexit $fail\n=== shared package ===\n{\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"enabled-engines\": [\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"vertx\"],\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"loan-payments.sqrl\",\n    \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": {\n        \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json\",\n        \"config\": {\n          \"is_batch\": false\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"api\": {\n      \"v1\": {\n        \"schema\": \"loan-payments-api/schema.v1.graphqls\",\n        \"operations\": [\"loan-payments-api/operations.v1.graphql\"]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\",\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n          \"enabled\": true,\n          \"defaultThresholdMs\": 200\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"compiler\": {\n    \"predicate-pushdown-rules\": \"LIMITED_RULES\",\n    \"api\": {\n      \"protocols\": [\"GRAPHQL\", \"REST\", \"MCP\"],\n      \"endpoints\": \"FULL\",\n      \"add-prefix\": true,\n      \"max-result-depth\": 2,\n      \"default-limit\": 20\n    }\n  }\n}\n=== prod package ===\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"prod\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"${AUTH_AUDIENCE}\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"${JWT_SECRET}\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n=== api test package ===\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"0 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"use-inferred-schema\": false,\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/loan-payments/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"loan-payments-api/tests/\"\n  }\n}\n=== table test package ===\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"0 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"use-inferred-schema\": true,\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/loan-payments_table_test/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"no-graphql-tests/\",\n    \"headers\": {\n      \"Authorization\": \"Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJsb2FuLXBheW1lbnRzLXRlc3QtaXNzdWVyIiwiYXVkIjpbImxvYW4tcGF5bWVudHMtdGVzdC1hdWRpZW5jZSJdLCJleHAiOjk5OTk5OTk5OTksImN1c3RvbWVyX2lkIjoiQ1VTVC0wNjAiLCJpYXQiOjE3OTU1MTEwMjZ9.PWHFR9DBUyp1W3WC7rQHPrNwmYiM20NDgc-TBjwd3Wc\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n=== README ===\n# Loan Payments\n\nA customer-facing data application for **personal loans**. It reads the shared finance\n[`data-catalog`](../data-catalog) sources `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment`, builds a payment\nhistory and a per-loan payment summary, and serves them through a JWT-authorized GraphQL, REST and\nMCP API. Each customer sees only the loans of the `customer_id` in their JWT.\n\nThe sub-project is `loan-payments`. The table tests run as a second sub-project,\n`loan-payments_table_test`.\n\n## Motivation and Requirements\n\nPersonal-loan customers sign in through the bank SSO and need to look up their own loans:\n\n- `LoanPayments`: one row per payment of a personal loan, with the customer and purpose of the\n  loan. It keeps payments in every status, so a customer still sees a returned payment.\n- `LoanPaymentSummary`: one row per personal loan, with payment counts, payment totals and the last\n  payment date. Only `APPLIED` payments contribute to the totals; a loan without payments still\n  appears with zero counts and a null last payment date.\n- Three lookups, all filtered by the JWT claim `customer_id`: a loan's summary, a loan's payments,\n  and every personal loan of the signed-in customer with its summary.\n\nAuto loans, mortgages and credit cards are out of scope, as are writes: the API has no mutation.\nThe pipeline manages no data of its own \u2014 it reuses the catalog connectors and its test data.\n\n## Data Sources\n\nBoth sources come from the shared `data-catalog` module, declared under `script.include` in\n`loan-payments-shared-package.json` and imported in `loan-payments.sqrl`. The project never copies or\nredeclares them.\n\n| Source | Test (`environment: test`) | Production (`environment: prod`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Personal_Loan` | `testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl` | Kafka topic `consumer_loans.personal_loan` |\n| `Loan_Payment` | `testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl` | Kafka topic `consumer_loans.loan_payment` |\n\nBoth are CDC streams with `ingested_at` watermarks (10 s in test, 30 s in prod). The project uses\nthe catalog watermarks as they are. The catalog's other consumer-loan tables \u2014 `Auto_Loan`,\n`Loan_Application` and `Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event` \u2014 are intentionally out of scope: the\nproject neither reads nor tests them.\n\n## Architecture and Implementation Choices\n\n| Table | What it does | In the API |\n|---|---|---|\n| `_LatestPersonalLoan` | latest version of each `Personal_Loan` by `source_updated_at`, key `loan_id`, all statuses | no |\n| `_LatestLoanPayment` \u2192 `_LatestPersonalLoanPayment` | latest version of each `Loan_Payment` by `source_updated_at`, key `payment_id`, filtered to `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` | no |\n| `LoanPaymentsSource` | personal payments inner-joined to their loan record, enriched with `customer_id` and `loan_purpose` | as the `LoanPayments` lookup |\n| `_AppliedPaymentAgg` / `_ReturnedPaymentAgg` | applied and returned payment counts and sums per loan | no |\n| `LoanPaymentSummarySource` | every latest personal loan left-joined to both aggregates | as the `LoanPaymentSummary` lookup |\n| `CustomerLoansSource` | the summary plus the loan details the client shows | as the `CustomerLoans` lookup |\n| `LoanPayments`, `LoanPaymentSummary`, `CustomerLoans` | claim-injected table functions over the sources above | yes |\n\n- **CDC dedup and no windows.** `DISTINCT ... ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC` keeps the latest\n  version per key, and the summary is an unwindowed, unbounded re-aggregation. A late or changed\n  payment updates the API within the freshness target (about one minute) instead of waiting for a\n  window boundary. The Flink state is bounded by key cardinality, not by time: the CDC dedup keeps\n  one state entry per loan and per payment, and the sources retain all payments, so state grows with\n  the number of loans and payments rather than with the stream length. At the assumed production\n  workload (up to 1,000,000 personal loans and 50,000 payments per day) the retained keys are large\n  but finite, and the \"no expiry\" retention requirement means state must grow with the retained\n  data rather than with the event rate. Windows would contradict the latest-status requirement,\n  which is why none are used. The state tables instead carry a derived event timestamp `_event_time`\n  (the greatest `ingested_at` of the rows that produced the state), so they keep valid event-time\n  semantics without a window.\n- **Loans without payments.** `LoanPaymentSummarySource` is anchored on the latest personal loans\n  and left-joins the applied-payment aggregate, so `PL-011` and the other payment-free loans appear\n  with zero counts and sums and a null `last_payment_date`. Grouping only the payments would omit\n  them.\n- **Flink + Postgres + Vert.x, no Kafka engine.** The dedup, enrichment and aggregation run in\n  Flink; the three lookups run in Postgres over indexed tables; Vert.x serves the API. There is no\n  mutation or subscription, so the `kafka` engine is not enabled \u2014 the catalog's Kafka sources are\n  ordinary external Flink connectors.\n- **Custom GraphQL schema.** `loan-payments-api/schema.v1.graphqls` fixes the exposed type and query\n  names, and `loan-payments-api/operations.v1.graphql` adds REST URIs and MCP tools. The claim\n  argument never appears as a caller argument.\n\n## API\n\n| Query | Arguments | Returns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)` | `loan_id` | one loan's summary, or `null` when the loan is not the caller's |\n| `LoanPayments(loan_id, limit, offset)` | `loan_id`, paging | the loan's payments, newest due date first |\n| `CustomerLoans(limit, offset)` | paging | every personal loan of the caller, each with its summary |\n\n`customer_id` is **not** an argument. It is read from the JWT and every query filters on it, so a\nrequest for another customer's loan returns no rows. GraphQL, REST and MCP are all exposed. Explicit\noperations: `LoanSummaryByLoan`, `LoanPaymentsByLoan`, `MyLoans`.\n\nThe API leaves out `loan_number` (`@npi @mask`), the account ids, the credit score and the DTI\nratio. Amounts stay in cents (`BIGINT`).\n\n## Authorization\n\nEvery query needs a valid JWT with the top-level claim `customer_id`; a request without a valid\ntoken is rejected (`JWT auth failed` / `Unauthorized`). The claim is mapped to a required function\nargument with `METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id'`.\n\nThe auth provider is configured under `engines.vertx.config.auth` in each test and prod overlay:\n\n- **test**: issuer `loan-payments-test-issuer`, audience `loan-payments-test-audience`, inline HS256\n  key from the fixed secret `dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string` (base64url without padding:\n  `ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw`).\n- **prod**: issuer `${AUTH_ISSUER}`, audience `${AUTH_AUDIENCE}`, key `${JWT_SECRET}` (base64url).\n\nMint a test token for any customer id:\n\n```bash\njwt encode --secret 'dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string' --alg HS256 \\\n  '{\"iss\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\", \"aud\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"], \"exp\": 9999999999, \"customer_id\": \"CUST-060\"}'\n```\n\n## How FR-8 Is Covered\n\nFR-8 says that when a payment changes status (for example `APPLIED` to `RETURNED`), the summary uses\nonly the latest status. It is covered **by construction**, not by a snapshot: `_LatestLoanPayment`\ndeduplicates on `payment_id` by `source_updated_at`, so only one version of each payment reaches\n`LoanPaymentsSource`; the unbounded aggregation then reads that single latest version. A changed\npayment therefore replaces its contribution to the counts and sums. No catalog payment changes\nstatus in the test data, and the scope forbids adding synthetic source data, so there is no\nsnapshot for it.\n\n## Data Quality and Observability\n\nThis project adds **no** pipeline-local `DQ_` or `OB_` tables:\n\n- The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from a `PERSONAL` payment to\n  `Personal_Loan`: a payment whose loan record never arrives would vanish. The catalog already\n  asserts referential integrity (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` in\n  `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`), so no capture is warranted.\n- The two sources are not independent senders, so ingestion counters add no signal; the catalog's\n  root-ontology snapshot queries already surface content changes.\n- The auto-loan exclusion is a required filter, not an anomaly, so it is not monitored.\n\nExisting coverage is reused from the catalog's lending-ontology assertions and root-ontology\nsnapshot queries.\n\n## Tests\n\nThe tests read the catalog test data through the catalog test connectors; the project adds no\nsource data. API tests and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses\none schema. The three public functions (`LoanPaymentSummary`, `LoanPayments`, `CustomerLoans`) are\nexercised through the custom schema by the API tests below, so their claim filter and ordering are\ncovered even though a `/*+test*/` table cannot inject the JWT claim:\n\n| Sub-project | Test package | Schema | Runs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `loan-payments` | `loan-payments-api-test-package.json` | custom (`use-inferred-schema: false`) | the API tests in `loan-payments-api/tests/` |\n| `loan-payments_table_test` | `loan-payments-table-test-package.json` | inferred (`use-inferred-schema: true`) | the `/*+test*/` tables in `loan-payments.sqrl` |\n\nAPI tests (each `X.graphql` with an optional `X.properties` token override):\n\n| Test | Token | Checks |\n|---|---|---|\n| `payment-returned` | `CUST-439` | `LoanPayments(\"PL-115\")` includes `LPMT-046` with status `RETURNED` and reason `R01` |\n| `loan-summary-returned` | `CUST-439` | `PL-115`: `returned_payment_count = 1`, `total_paid_cents = 635360` (the returned 57,760 is excluded) |\n| `loan-late-fees` | `CUST-061` | `PL-035`: `late_fees_paid_cents = 7500` |\n| `zero-payment-loan` | `CUST-066` | `PL-011`: zero counts and sums, null `last_payment_date` |\n| `customer-loans` | `CUST-060` | the four loans `PL-006`, `PL-007`, `PL-022`, `PL-033`, each with its summary |\n| `wrong-customer-summary` | `CUST-060` | `LoanPaymentSummary(\"PL-115\")` returns `null` |\n| `wrong-customer-payments` | `CUST-060` | `LoanPayments(\"PL-115\")` returns no rows |\n| `missing-token` | none | the request is rejected with an authorization error |\n\nTable tests:\n\n| Test | Checks |\n|---|---|\n| `LoanPaymentsCountTest` | 1,010 personal payment rows over 135 loans |\n| `AutoLoanLeakTest` (`no_rows`) | no `AL-` loan id leaked in |\n| `DuplicateLoanPaymentsTest` (`no_rows`) | one row per `payment_id` |\n| `DuplicateLoanSummaryTest` (`no_rows`) | one row per `loan_id` |\n| `ZeroPaymentSummaryTest` | a payment-free loan has zero counts/sums and a null last payment date |\n| `CustomerLoansSourceTest` | the four-loan customer `CUST-060` appears with one row per loan and its summary |\n\nExpected results are in `snapshots/loan-payments/` and `snapshots/loan-payments_table_test/`.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\nloan-payments/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments.sqrl                        # processing, lookups and table tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-shared-package.json         # base config: engines, catalog include, API\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-api-test-package.json       # custom-schema API tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-table-test-package.json     # inferred-schema table tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-prod-package.json           # production auth\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-api/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                    # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql                 # REST/MCP operations\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                                # API test queries and tokens\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments/                        # API snapshots\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 loan-payments_table_test/             # table snapshots\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n\n## Commands\n\nThe `./run-tests.sh` commands run from the project folder, which the `cd` line enters from the root\nof the repository.\n\n```bash\ncd loan-payments                             # the project folder, from the repository root\n./run-tests.sh                               # run the API tests and the table tests\n./run-tests.sh loan-payments                 # run only the API tests\n./run-tests.sh loan-payments_table_test      # run only the table tests\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod loan-payments   # compile the production pipeline\n```\n\nThe `docker run` commands make the same DataSQRL calls and work from any folder inside the git\nrepository.\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  compile -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-prod-package.json -b loan-payments\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json -b loan-payments\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json -b loan-payments_table_test\n```\n\n`-b <name>` writes the output to `build/<name>`, and it is optional.\n\nThe project has no `dev` environment; the `test` environment reads the catalog's filesystem test data\nand can be used for a local run:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -it --rm -p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081 -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  run -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json\n```\n\nWhile the pipeline runs: the Flink WebUI is at http://localhost:8081/, the GraphQL API and GraphiQL\nat http://localhost:8888/v1/graphiql/, the REST Swagger UI at http://localhost:8888/v1/swagger-ui,\nthe OpenAPI JSON at http://localhost:8888/v1/openapi, and the MCP API at\n`http://localhost:8888/v1/mcp/`. Every request needs a token from the [Authorization](#authorization)\nsection.\n\nA production deployment needs `AUTH_ISSUER`, `AUTH_AUDIENCE` and `JWT_SECRET`, and the catalog\nrequires `LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS`.\n"}], "details": {}}}
{"request_uuid": "caacdd4d-ad99-478a-ba94-72fe26930973", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T14:41:55.607853+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "patch", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20e01989e76138e90e2e5", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "=== run-tests.sh ===\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# This project's suites \u2014 one command, deterministic exit code.\n# Used by the DataSQRL agent and by CI.\n#\n# USAGE\n#   ./run-tests.sh [options] [<sub-project> ...]\n#\n#   <sub-project>       zero or more names, in any order, selecting which suites run.\n#                       Give none to run them all. Options may appear before, after or\n#                       between the names.\n#\n#   --compile           compile instead of running tests\n#   --test              run tests (the default)\n#   --env <name>        which environment's configs to use (default: test).\n#                       `--env=<name>` is accepted too.\n#   --list-envs         print the environments this project declares, one line\n#   --list-subprojects  print the sub-projects this project ships, one line\n#   --list-invocations  print what would run and run nothing, one\n#                       `SQRL_INVOCATION <sub-project> <verb> -r <root> <config...> -b <name>`\n#                       line per suite \u2014 exactly the arguments a real run would use\n#\n# EXAMPLES (using this project's own sub-project, event_audit)\n#   ./run-tests.sh                                     test every suite, env test\n#   ./run-tests.sh event_audit                         test just that sub-project\n#   ./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev                 compile every suite as dev\n#   ./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod event_audit    compile just that one, as prod\n#   ./run-tests.sh --list-invocations --compile --env dev     show what would run\n#\n#   A project with several sub-projects names as many as it likes:\n#   ./run-tests.sh <sub-project> <sub-project>\n#\n# An unknown sub-project name, or an environment this project does not declare, is an error\n# (exit 2) \u2014 never a silent no-op that runs nothing and reports success.\n#\n# WRITING THIS FILE FOR A PROJECT\n#   Three blocks below are marked \"EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT\": the ENVS/SUBPROJECTS declarations,\n#   the `suite` table, and the optional setup/teardown hooks. Everything else is boilerplate \u2014\n#   copy it verbatim.\n#\n#   The `suite` table is the ONE place a project's config layering is written down: for each\n#   sub-project and each environment, the exact configs to layer, in merge order.\n#\nset -uo pipefail\n# `set -e` (exit on the first error) is not used to allow for more granular error handling.\n\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\"\n\n# Where this project sits relative to the compiler's mount root. Inside the DataSQRL agent the mount\n# root is the repository, so a project in a subdirectory is reached with the prefix the\n# orchestrator exports; empty everywhere else, meaning the project IS the mount root.\n#\n# It is passed as `-r <root>` rather than glued onto every config path. Both forms are equivalent\n# to the compiler, but `-r` is REQUIRED once `-b` points inside build/: without it the compiler\n# resolves script.main against the wrong root and fails with \"Could not find main SQRL script\".\nP=\"${SQRL_PACKAGE_PREFIX:-}\"\nif [ -n \"$P\" ]; then\n  ROOT=\"${P%/}\"                                   # the DataSQRL agent told us where we are\nelif ROOT=\"$(git rev-parse --show-prefix 2>/dev/null)\"; then\n  ROOT=\"${ROOT%/}\"; ROOT=\"${ROOT:-.}\"             # a repo checkout: our path within it\nelse\n  ROOT=\".\"                                        # not in a repo: this project is the root\nfi\n\n# The DataSQRL CLI version the DataSQRL agent container ships; CI and local runs default to the same\n# one so snapshots match. Override with SQRL_VERSION.\nSQRL_VERSION=\"${SQRL_VERSION:-0.12.5}\"\n# Fallback tag for when that version is not published yet (a release in flight). Resolved once,\n# on the first docker invocation, and never inside the DataSQRL agent \u2014 the container has its own CLI.\nSQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE=\"${SQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE:-datasqrl/cmd:dev}\"\nIMAGE=\"\"\n\nresolve_image() {\n  [ -n \"$IMAGE\" ] && return 0\n  local want=\"datasqrl/cmd:${SQRL_VERSION}\"\n  if docker image inspect \"$want\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || docker pull -q \"$want\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    IMAGE=\"$want\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n  IMAGE=\"$SQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE\"\n  # Worth a warning, not a failure: snapshots were generated with the pinned version, so a\n  # different CLI can legitimately produce different output and fail tests that are fine.\n  printf 'warning: datasqrl/cmd:%s is not available; falling back to %s\\n' \\\n    \"$SQRL_VERSION\" \"$IMAGE\" >&2\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# The environments this project's configs cover, and the sub-projects it ships. Both are\n# declared rather than discovered, so that asking for something this project does not have\n# fails loudly instead of matching nothing, running zero suites and still exiting 0.\nENVS=\"test prod\"\nSUBPROJECTS=\"loan-payments loan-payments_table_test\"\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nVERB=test\nENV=test\nLIST=\"\"\nSELECTED=\"\"\nwhile [ $# -gt 0 ]; do\n  case \"$1\" in\n    --compile)   VERB=compile ;;\n    --test)      VERB=test ;;\n    --env)       ENV=\"${2:-}\"; shift ;;\n    --env=*)     ENV=\"${1#--env=}\" ;;\n    --list-invocations) LIST=1 ;;\n    --list-envs) echo \"$ENVS\"; exit 0 ;;\n    --list-subprojects) echo \"$SUBPROJECTS\"; exit 0 ;;\n    -*)          echo \"unknown option: $1\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n    *)           SELECTED=\"$SELECTED $1\" ;;\n  esac\n  shift\ndone\ncase \" $ENVS \" in\n  *\" $ENV \"*) ;;\n  *) echo \"unknown env '$ENV' \u2014 this project declares: $ENVS\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\nesac\n\nfor s in $SELECTED; do\n  case \" $SUBPROJECTS \" in\n    *\" $s \"*) ;;\n    *) echo \"unknown sub-project '$s' \u2014 this project ships: $SUBPROJECTS\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n  esac\ndone\n\nselected() {\n  [ -z \"$SELECTED\" ] && return 0\n  case \" $SELECTED \" in *\" $1 \"*) return 0 ;; esac\n  return 1\n}\n\n# Each suite writes its own build folder (the -b flag in the driver loop below), so one suite's\n# artifacts never overwrite a sibling's. `-b <name>` names a subfolder of build/: DataSQRL\n# resolves `-b fraud_store` to <project-root>/build/fraud_store\n\nsqrl() {\n  local verb=\"$1\"; shift\n  # Every named config must exist. A missing one means the suite line and this environment\n  # disagree, and compiling a partial argument list would quietly review the wrong thing.\n  local a missing=0\n  for a in \"$@\"; do\n    case \"$a\" in\n      *.json) [ -f \"$a\" ] || { echo \"missing config: $a\" >&2; missing=1; } ;;\n    esac\n  done\n  [ \"$missing\" = 1 ] && return 1\n  # List mode: report what would run and return. Bash has already expanded the environment and\n  # the path prefix, so the caller reads the real argument list without parsing this file. The\n  # sub-project is named first so each line stands on its own.\n  if [ -n \"$LIST\" ]; then\n    printf 'SQRL_INVOCATION %s %s -r %s %s\\n' \"$SUITE\" \"$verb\" \"$ROOT\" \"$*\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n\n  if [ -x /opt/agent/cmd.sh ]; then\n    /opt/agent/cmd.sh \"$verb\" -r \"$ROOT\" \"$@\"     # inside the DataSQRL agent container\n    return\n  fi\n  # CI / local developer machine: run the DataSQRL CLI image. Mount the REPOSITORY root at\n  # /workspace (not just this project) so a sibling shared module this project consumes exists\n  # inside the container too; `-r <project>` then points the compiler at this project.\n  local root\n  root=\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)\" || root=\"$PWD\"\n  # The image runs in UTC, and every template's snapshots are generated in UTC. The container's\n  # default is left alone. TZ passes through only when set explicitly.\n  resolve_image\n  docker run --rm ${TZ:+-e TZ=\"$TZ\"} -v \"$root\":/workspace \\\n    \"$IMAGE\" \"$verb\" -r \"$ROOT\" \"$@\"\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT (optional): setup \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# The test sources are the read-only `../data-catalog` submodule's\n# lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/*.jsonl fixtures. The DataSQRL test runner sets ${DATA_PATH}\n# to that testdata folder. On a clean checkout initialize the submodule; in the agent container it\n# is already mounted, so the guard skips the fetch.\nif [ -z \"$LIST\" ] && [ ! -d ../data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata ]; then\n  git submodule update --init --recursive || exit 1\nfi\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT: the suite table \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# One line per (sub-project, environment), naming that combination's configs in merge order \u2014\n# base first, environment config last, since a later file overrides an earlier one. Write the\n# filenames out literally; any names work. Cover every pair of SUBPROJECTS x ENVS that this\n# project actually has, and leave out the ones it does not: asking for a pair that is missing\n# reports an error rather than compiling something half-declared.\n#\n#   <sub-project>/<env>) echo \"<base config> <env config>\" ;;\n#\nsuite() {\n  case \"$1/$2\" in\n    loan-payments/test)             echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json\" ;;\n    loan-payments/prod)             echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-prod-package.json\" ;;\n    loan-payments_table_test/test)  echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json\" ;;\n    loan-payments_table_test/prod)  echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json\" ;;\n    *) return 1 ;;\n  esac\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# Every selected sub-project runs even after one fails, so a single run reports every failure.\nfail=0\nSUITE=\"\"\nfor sub in $SUBPROJECTS; do\n  selected \"$sub\" || continue\n  SUITE=\"$sub\"\n  args=\"$(suite \"$sub\" \"$ENV\")\" || {\n    echo \"no '$ENV' configs declared for sub-project '$sub'\" >&2\n    fail=1\n    continue\n  }\n  # shellcheck disable=SC2086  # the declaration is a space-separated argument list, on purpose\n  sqrl \"$VERB\" $args -b \"$sub\" || fail=1\ndone\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT (optional): teardown \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# [ -n \"$LIST\" ] || ./scripts/teardown.sh\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nexit $fail\n=== shared package ===\n{\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"enabled-engines\": [\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"vertx\"],\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"loan-payments.sqrl\",\n    \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": {\n        \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json\",\n        \"config\": {\n          \"is_batch\": false\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"api\": {\n      \"v1\": {\n        \"schema\": \"loan-payments-api/schema.v1.graphqls\",\n        \"operations\": [\"loan-payments-api/operations.v1.graphql\"]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\",\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n          \"enabled\": true,\n          \"defaultThresholdMs\": 200\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"compiler\": {\n    \"predicate-pushdown-rules\": \"LIMITED_RULES\",\n    \"api\": {\n      \"protocols\": [\"GRAPHQL\", \"REST\", \"MCP\"],\n      \"endpoints\": \"FULL\",\n      \"add-prefix\": true,\n      \"max-result-depth\": 2,\n      \"default-limit\": 20\n    }\n  }\n}\n=== prod package ===\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"prod\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"${AUTH_AUDIENCE}\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"${JWT_SECRET}\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n=== api test package ===\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"0 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"use-inferred-schema\": false,\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/loan-payments/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"loan-payments-api/tests/\"\n  }\n}\n=== table test package ===\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"0 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"use-inferred-schema\": true,\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/loan-payments_table_test/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"no-graphql-tests/\",\n    \"headers\": {\n      \"Authorization\": \"Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJsb2FuLXBheW1lbnRzLXRlc3QtaXNzdWVyIiwiYXVkIjpbImxvYW4tcGF5bWVudHMtdGVzdC1hdWRpZW5jZSJdLCJleHAiOjk5OTk5OTk5OTksImN1c3RvbWVyX2lkIjoiQ1VTVC0wNjAiLCJpYXQiOjE3OTU1MTEwMjZ9.PWHFR9DBUyp1W3WC7rQHPrNwmYiM20NDgc-TBjwd3Wc\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n=== README ===\n# Loan Payments\n\nA customer-facing data application for **personal loans**. It reads the shared finance\n[`data-catalog`](../data-catalog) sources `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment`, builds a payment\nhistory and a per-loan payment summary, and serves them through a JWT-authorized GraphQL, REST and\nMCP API. Each customer sees only the loans of the `customer_id` in their JWT.\n\nThe sub-project is `loan-payments`. The table tests run as a second sub-project,\n`loan-payments_table_test`.\n\n## Motivation and Requirements\n\nPersonal-loan customers sign in through the bank SSO and need to look up their own loans:\n\n- `LoanPayments`: one row per payment of a personal loan, with the customer and purpose of the\n  loan. It keeps payments in every status, so a customer still sees a returned payment.\n- `LoanPaymentSummary`: one row per personal loan, with payment counts, payment totals and the last\n  payment date. Only `APPLIED` payments contribute to the totals; a loan without payments still\n  appears with zero counts and a null last payment date.\n- Three lookups, all filtered by the JWT claim `customer_id`: a loan's summary, a loan's payments,\n  and every personal loan of the signed-in customer with its summary.\n\nAuto loans, mortgages and credit cards are out of scope, as are writes: the API has no mutation.\nThe pipeline manages no data of its own \u2014 it reuses the catalog connectors and its test data.\n\n## Data Sources\n\nBoth sources come from the shared `data-catalog` module, declared under `script.include` in\n`loan-payments-shared-package.json` and imported in `loan-payments.sqrl`. The project never copies or\nredeclares them.\n\n| Source | Test (`environment: test`) | Production (`environment: prod`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Personal_Loan` | `testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl` | Kafka topic `consumer_loans.personal_loan` |\n| `Loan_Payment` | `testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl` | Kafka topic `consumer_loans.loan_payment` |\n\nBoth are CDC streams with `ingested_at` watermarks (10 s in test, 30 s in prod). The project uses\nthe catalog watermarks as they are. The catalog's other consumer-loan tables \u2014 `Auto_Loan`,\n`Loan_Application` and `Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event` \u2014 are intentionally out of scope: the\nproject neither reads nor tests them.\n\n## Architecture and Implementation Choices\n\n| Table | What it does | In the API |\n|---|---|---|\n| `_LatestPersonalLoan` | latest version of each `Personal_Loan` by `source_updated_at`, key `loan_id`, all statuses | no |\n| `_LatestLoanPayment` \u2192 `_LatestPersonalLoanPayment` | latest version of each `Loan_Payment` by `source_updated_at`, key `payment_id`, filtered to `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` | no |\n| `LoanPaymentsSource` | personal payments inner-joined to their loan record, enriched with `customer_id` and `loan_purpose` | as the `LoanPayments` lookup |\n| `_AppliedPaymentAgg` / `_ReturnedPaymentAgg` | applied and returned payment counts and sums per loan | no |\n| `LoanPaymentSummarySource` | every latest personal loan left-joined to both aggregates | as the `LoanPaymentSummary` lookup |\n| `CustomerLoansSource` | the summary plus the loan details the client shows | as the `CustomerLoans` lookup |\n| `LoanPayments`, `LoanPaymentSummary`, `CustomerLoans` | claim-injected table functions over the sources above | yes |\n\n- **CDC dedup and no windows.** `DISTINCT ... ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC` keeps the latest\n  version per key, and the summary is an unwindowed, unbounded re-aggregation. A late or changed\n  payment updates the API within the freshness target (about one minute) instead of waiting for a\n  window boundary. The Flink state is bounded by key cardinality, not by time: the CDC dedup keeps\n  one state entry per loan and per payment, and the sources retain all payments, so state grows with\n  the number of loans and payments rather than with the stream length. At the assumed production\n  workload (up to 1,000,000 personal loans and 50,000 payments per day) the retained keys are large\n  but finite, and the \"no expiry\" retention requirement means state must grow with the retained\n  data rather than with the event rate. Windows would contradict the latest-status requirement,\n  which is why none are used. The state tables instead carry a derived event timestamp `_event_time`\n  (the greatest `ingested_at` of the rows that produced the state), so they keep valid event-time\n  semantics without a window.\n- **Loans without payments.** `LoanPaymentSummarySource` is anchored on the latest personal loans\n  and left-joins the applied-payment aggregate, so `PL-011` and the other payment-free loans appear\n  with zero counts and sums and a null `last_payment_date`. Grouping only the payments would omit\n  them.\n- **Flink + Postgres + Vert.x, no Kafka engine.** The dedup, enrichment and aggregation run in\n  Flink; the three lookups run in Postgres over indexed tables; Vert.x serves the API. There is no\n  mutation or subscription, so the `kafka` engine is not enabled \u2014 the catalog's Kafka sources are\n  ordinary external Flink connectors.\n- **Custom GraphQL schema.** `loan-payments-api/schema.v1.graphqls` fixes the exposed type and query\n  names, and `loan-payments-api/operations.v1.graphql` adds REST URIs and MCP tools. The claim\n  argument never appears as a caller argument.\n\n## API\n\n| Query | Arguments | Returns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)` | `loan_id` | one loan's summary, or `null` when the loan is not the caller's |\n| `LoanPayments(loan_id, limit, offset)` | `loan_id`, paging | the loan's payments, newest due date first |\n| `CustomerLoans(limit, offset)` | paging | every personal loan of the caller, each with its summary |\n\n`customer_id` is **not** an argument. It is read from the JWT and every query filters on it, so a\nrequest for another customer's loan returns no rows. GraphQL, REST and MCP are all exposed. Explicit\noperations: `LoanSummaryByLoan`, `LoanPaymentsByLoan`, `MyLoans`.\n\nThe API leaves out `loan_number` (`@npi @mask`), the account ids, the credit score and the DTI\nratio. Amounts stay in cents (`BIGINT`).\n\n## Authorization\n\nEvery query needs a valid JWT with the top-level claim `customer_id`; a request without a valid\ntoken is rejected (`JWT auth failed` / `Unauthorized`). The claim is mapped to a required function\nargument with `METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id'`.\n\nThe auth provider is configured under `engines.vertx.config.auth` in each test and prod overlay:\n\n- **test**: issuer `loan-payments-test-issuer`, audience `loan-payments-test-audience`, inline HS256\n  key from the fixed secret `dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string` (base64url without padding:\n  `ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw`).\n- **prod**: issuer `${AUTH_ISSUER}`, audience `${AUTH_AUDIENCE}`, key `${JWT_SECRET}` (base64url).\n\nMint a test token for any customer id:\n\n```bash\njwt encode --secret 'dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string' --alg HS256 \\\n  '{\"iss\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\", \"aud\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"], \"exp\": 9999999999, \"customer_id\": \"CUST-060\"}'\n```\n\n## How FR-8 Is Covered\n\nFR-8 says that when a payment changes status (for example `APPLIED` to `RETURNED`), the summary uses\nonly the latest status. It is covered **by construction**, not by a snapshot: `_LatestLoanPayment`\ndeduplicates on `payment_id` by `source_updated_at`, so only one version of each payment reaches\n`LoanPaymentsSource`; the unbounded aggregation then reads that single latest version. A changed\npayment therefore replaces its contribution to the counts and sums. No catalog payment changes\nstatus in the test data, and the scope forbids adding synthetic source data, so there is no\nsnapshot for it.\n\n## Data Quality and Observability\n\nThis project adds **no** pipeline-local `DQ_` or `OB_` tables:\n\n- The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from a `PERSONAL` payment to\n  `Personal_Loan`: a payment whose loan record never arrives would vanish. The catalog already\n  asserts referential integrity (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` in\n  `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`), so no capture is warranted.\n- The two sources are not independent senders, so ingestion counters add no signal; the catalog's\n  root-ontology snapshot queries already surface content changes.\n- The auto-loan exclusion is a required filter, not an anomaly, so it is not monitored.\n\nExisting coverage is reused from the catalog's lending-ontology assertions and root-ontology\nsnapshot queries.\n\n## Tests\n\nThe tests read the catalog test data through the catalog test connectors; the project adds no\nsource data. API tests and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses\none schema. The three public functions (`LoanPaymentSummary`, `LoanPayments`, `CustomerLoans`) are\nexercised through the custom schema by the API tests below, so their claim filter and ordering are\ncovered even though a `/*+test*/` table cannot inject the JWT claim:\n\n| Sub-project | Test package | Schema | Runs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `loan-payments` | `loan-payments-api-test-package.json` | custom (`use-inferred-schema: false`) | the API tests in `loan-payments-api/tests/` |\n| `loan-payments_table_test` | `loan-payments-table-test-package.json` | inferred (`use-inferred-schema: true`) | the `/*+test*/` tables in `loan-payments.sqrl` |\n\nAPI tests (each `X.graphql` with an optional `X.properties` token override):\n\n| Test | Token | Checks |\n|---|---|---|\n| `payment-returned` | `CUST-439` | `LoanPayments(\"PL-115\")` includes `LPMT-046` with status `RETURNED` and reason `R01` |\n| `loan-summary-returned` | `CUST-439` | `PL-115`: `returned_payment_count = 1`, `total_paid_cents = 635360` (the returned 57,760 is excluded) |\n| `loan-late-fees` | `CUST-061` | `PL-035`: `late_fees_paid_cents = 7500` |\n| `zero-payment-loan` | `CUST-066` | `PL-011`: zero counts and sums, null `last_payment_date` |\n| `customer-loans` | `CUST-060` | the four loans `PL-006`, `PL-007`, `PL-022`, `PL-033`, each with its summary |\n| `wrong-customer-summary` | `CUST-060` | `LoanPaymentSummary(\"PL-115\")` returns `null` |\n| `wrong-customer-payments` | `CUST-060` | `LoanPayments(\"PL-115\")` returns no rows |\n| `missing-token` | none | the request is rejected with an authorization error |\n\nTable tests:\n\n| Test | Checks |\n|---|---|\n| `LoanPaymentsCountTest` | 1,010 personal payment rows over 135 loans |\n| `AutoLoanLeakTest` (`no_rows`) | no `AL-` loan id leaked in |\n| `DuplicateLoanPaymentsTest` (`no_rows`) | one row per `payment_id` |\n| `DuplicateLoanSummaryTest` (`no_rows`) | one row per `loan_id` |\n| `ZeroPaymentSummaryTest` | a payment-free loan has zero counts/sums and a null last payment date |\n| `CustomerLoansSourceTest` | the four-loan customer `CUST-060` appears with one row per loan and its summary |\n\nExpected results are in `snapshots/loan-payments/` and `snapshots/loan-payments_table_test/`.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\nloan-payments/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments.sqrl                        # processing, lookups and table tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-shared-package.json         # base config: engines, catalog include, API\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-api-test-package.json       # custom-schema API tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-table-test-package.json     # inferred-schema table tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-prod-package.json           # production auth\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-api/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                    # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql                 # REST/MCP operations\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                                # API test queries and tokens\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments/                        # API snapshots\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 loan-payments_table_test/             # table snapshots\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n\n## Commands\n\nThe `./run-tests.sh` commands run from the project folder, which the `cd` line enters from the root\nof the repository.\n\n```bash\ncd loan-payments                             # the project folder, from the repository root\n./run-tests.sh                               # run the API tests and the table tests\n./run-tests.sh loan-payments                 # run only the API tests\n./run-tests.sh loan-payments_table_test      # run only the table tests\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod loan-payments   # compile the production pipeline\n```\n\nThe `docker run` commands make the same DataSQRL calls and work from any folder inside the git\nrepository.\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  compile -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-prod-package.json -b loan-payments\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json -b loan-payments\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json -b loan-payments_table_test\n```\n\n`-b <name>` writes the output to `build/<name>`, and it is optional.\n\nThe project has no `dev` environment; the `test` environment reads the catalog's filesystem test data\nand can be used for a local run:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -it --rm -p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081 -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  run -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json\n```\n\nWhile the pipeline runs: the Flink WebUI is at http://localhost:8081/, the GraphQL API and GraphiQL\nat http://localhost:8888/v1/graphiql/, the REST Swagger UI at http://localhost:8888/v1/swagger-ui,\nthe OpenAPI JSON at http://localhost:8888/v1/openapi, and the MCP API at\n`http://localhost:8888/v1/mcp/`. Every request needs a token from the [Authorization](#authorization)\nsection.\n\nA production deployment needs `AUTH_ISSUER`, `AUTH_AUDIENCE` and `JWT_SECRET`, and the catalog\nrequires `LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS`.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 13, "timestamp": 1795531315606}}
{"request_uuid": "caacdd4d-ad99-478a-ba94-72fe26930973", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T14:41:55.608427+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "patch", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_end", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me read the project files.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20e01989e76138e90e2e5", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/loan-payments && echo \"=== run-tests.sh ===\" && cat run-tests.sh && echo \"=== shared package ===\" && cat loan-payments-shared-package.json && echo \"=== prod package ===\" && cat loan-payments-prod-package.json && echo \"=== api test package ===\" && cat loan-payments-api-test-package.json && echo \"=== table test package ===\" && cat loan-payments-table-test-package.json && echo \"=== README ===\" && cat README.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 15369, "output": 155, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 7, "totalTokens": 15524, "cost": {"input": 0.0046107, "output": 0.000186, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0047967}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795531314128, "responseId": "e2b95eb4f93b4878a7011637f43f2690-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1462, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, "toolResults": [{"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20e01989e76138e90e2e5", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "=== run-tests.sh ===\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# This project's suites \u2014 one command, deterministic exit code.\n# Used by the DataSQRL agent and by CI.\n#\n# USAGE\n#   ./run-tests.sh [options] [<sub-project> ...]\n#\n#   <sub-project>       zero or more names, in any order, selecting which suites run.\n#                       Give none to run them all. Options may appear before, after or\n#                       between the names.\n#\n#   --compile           compile instead of running tests\n#   --test              run tests (the default)\n#   --env <name>        which environment's configs to use (default: test).\n#                       `--env=<name>` is accepted too.\n#   --list-envs         print the environments this project declares, one line\n#   --list-subprojects  print the sub-projects this project ships, one line\n#   --list-invocations  print what would run and run nothing, one\n#                       `SQRL_INVOCATION <sub-project> <verb> -r <root> <config...> -b <name>`\n#                       line per suite \u2014 exactly the arguments a real run would use\n#\n# EXAMPLES (using this project's own sub-project, event_audit)\n#   ./run-tests.sh                                     test every suite, env test\n#   ./run-tests.sh event_audit                         test just that sub-project\n#   ./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev                 compile every suite as dev\n#   ./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod event_audit    compile just that one, as prod\n#   ./run-tests.sh --list-invocations --compile --env dev     show what would run\n#\n#   A project with several sub-projects names as many as it likes:\n#   ./run-tests.sh <sub-project> <sub-project>\n#\n# An unknown sub-project name, or an environment this project does not declare, is an error\n# (exit 2) \u2014 never a silent no-op that runs nothing and reports success.\n#\n# WRITING THIS FILE FOR A PROJECT\n#   Three blocks below are marked \"EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT\": the ENVS/SUBPROJECTS declarations,\n#   the `suite` table, and the optional setup/teardown hooks. Everything else is boilerplate \u2014\n#   copy it verbatim.\n#\n#   The `suite` table is the ONE place a project's config layering is written down: for each\n#   sub-project and each environment, the exact configs to layer, in merge order.\n#\nset -uo pipefail\n# `set -e` (exit on the first error) is not used to allow for more granular error handling.\n\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\"\n\n# Where this project sits relative to the compiler's mount root. Inside the DataSQRL agent the mount\n# root is the repository, so a project in a subdirectory is reached with the prefix the\n# orchestrator exports; empty everywhere else, meaning the project IS the mount root.\n#\n# It is passed as `-r <root>` rather than glued onto every config path. Both forms are equivalent\n# to the compiler, but `-r` is REQUIRED once `-b` points inside build/: without it the compiler\n# resolves script.main against the wrong root and fails with \"Could not find main SQRL script\".\nP=\"${SQRL_PACKAGE_PREFIX:-}\"\nif [ -n \"$P\" ]; then\n  ROOT=\"${P%/}\"                                   # the DataSQRL agent told us where we are\nelif ROOT=\"$(git rev-parse --show-prefix 2>/dev/null)\"; then\n  ROOT=\"${ROOT%/}\"; ROOT=\"${ROOT:-.}\"             # a repo checkout: our path within it\nelse\n  ROOT=\".\"                                        # not in a repo: this project is the root\nfi\n\n# The DataSQRL CLI version the DataSQRL agent container ships; CI and local runs default to the same\n# one so snapshots match. Override with SQRL_VERSION.\nSQRL_VERSION=\"${SQRL_VERSION:-0.12.5}\"\n# Fallback tag for when that version is not published yet (a release in flight). Resolved once,\n# on the first docker invocation, and never inside the DataSQRL agent \u2014 the container has its own CLI.\nSQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE=\"${SQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE:-datasqrl/cmd:dev}\"\nIMAGE=\"\"\n\nresolve_image() {\n  [ -n \"$IMAGE\" ] && return 0\n  local want=\"datasqrl/cmd:${SQRL_VERSION}\"\n  if docker image inspect \"$want\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || docker pull -q \"$want\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    IMAGE=\"$want\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n  IMAGE=\"$SQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE\"\n  # Worth a warning, not a failure: snapshots were generated with the pinned version, so a\n  # different CLI can legitimately produce different output and fail tests that are fine.\n  printf 'warning: datasqrl/cmd:%s is not available; falling back to %s\\n' \\\n    \"$SQRL_VERSION\" \"$IMAGE\" >&2\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# The environments this project's configs cover, and the sub-projects it ships. Both are\n# declared rather than discovered, so that asking for something this project does not have\n# fails loudly instead of matching nothing, running zero suites and still exiting 0.\nENVS=\"test prod\"\nSUBPROJECTS=\"loan-payments loan-payments_table_test\"\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nVERB=test\nENV=test\nLIST=\"\"\nSELECTED=\"\"\nwhile [ $# -gt 0 ]; do\n  case \"$1\" in\n    --compile)   VERB=compile ;;\n    --test)      VERB=test ;;\n    --env)       ENV=\"${2:-}\"; shift ;;\n    --env=*)     ENV=\"${1#--env=}\" ;;\n    --list-invocations) LIST=1 ;;\n    --list-envs) echo \"$ENVS\"; exit 0 ;;\n    --list-subprojects) echo \"$SUBPROJECTS\"; exit 0 ;;\n    -*)          echo \"unknown option: $1\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n    *)           SELECTED=\"$SELECTED $1\" ;;\n  esac\n  shift\ndone\ncase \" $ENVS \" in\n  *\" $ENV \"*) ;;\n  *) echo \"unknown env '$ENV' \u2014 this project declares: $ENVS\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\nesac\n\nfor s in $SELECTED; do\n  case \" $SUBPROJECTS \" in\n    *\" $s \"*) ;;\n    *) echo \"unknown sub-project '$s' \u2014 this project ships: $SUBPROJECTS\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n  esac\ndone\n\nselected() {\n  [ -z \"$SELECTED\" ] && return 0\n  case \" $SELECTED \" in *\" $1 \"*) return 0 ;; esac\n  return 1\n}\n\n# Each suite writes its own build folder (the -b flag in the driver loop below), so one suite's\n# artifacts never overwrite a sibling's. `-b <name>` names a subfolder of build/: DataSQRL\n# resolves `-b fraud_store` to <project-root>/build/fraud_store\n\nsqrl() {\n  local verb=\"$1\"; shift\n  # Every named config must exist. A missing one means the suite line and this environment\n  # disagree, and compiling a partial argument list would quietly review the wrong thing.\n  local a missing=0\n  for a in \"$@\"; do\n    case \"$a\" in\n      *.json) [ -f \"$a\" ] || { echo \"missing config: $a\" >&2; missing=1; } ;;\n    esac\n  done\n  [ \"$missing\" = 1 ] && return 1\n  # List mode: report what would run and return. Bash has already expanded the environment and\n  # the path prefix, so the caller reads the real argument list without parsing this file. The\n  # sub-project is named first so each line stands on its own.\n  if [ -n \"$LIST\" ]; then\n    printf 'SQRL_INVOCATION %s %s -r %s %s\\n' \"$SUITE\" \"$verb\" \"$ROOT\" \"$*\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n\n  if [ -x /opt/agent/cmd.sh ]; then\n    /opt/agent/cmd.sh \"$verb\" -r \"$ROOT\" \"$@\"     # inside the DataSQRL agent container\n    return\n  fi\n  # CI / local developer machine: run the DataSQRL CLI image. Mount the REPOSITORY root at\n  # /workspace (not just this project) so a sibling shared module this project consumes exists\n  # inside the container too; `-r <project>` then points the compiler at this project.\n  local root\n  root=\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)\" || root=\"$PWD\"\n  # The image runs in UTC, and every template's snapshots are generated in UTC. The container's\n  # default is left alone. TZ passes through only when set explicitly.\n  resolve_image\n  docker run --rm ${TZ:+-e TZ=\"$TZ\"} -v \"$root\":/workspace \\\n    \"$IMAGE\" \"$verb\" -r \"$ROOT\" \"$@\"\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT (optional): setup \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# The test sources are the read-only `../data-catalog` submodule's\n# lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/*.jsonl fixtures. The DataSQRL test runner sets ${DATA_PATH}\n# to that testdata folder. On a clean checkout initialize the submodule; in the agent container it\n# is already mounted, so the guard skips the fetch.\nif [ -z \"$LIST\" ] && [ ! -d ../data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata ]; then\n  git submodule update --init --recursive || exit 1\nfi\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT: the suite table \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# One line per (sub-project, environment), naming that combination's configs in merge order \u2014\n# base first, environment config last, since a later file overrides an earlier one. Write the\n# filenames out literally; any names work. Cover every pair of SUBPROJECTS x ENVS that this\n# project actually has, and leave out the ones it does not: asking for a pair that is missing\n# reports an error rather than compiling something half-declared.\n#\n#   <sub-project>/<env>) echo \"<base config> <env config>\" ;;\n#\nsuite() {\n  case \"$1/$2\" in\n    loan-payments/test)             echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json\" ;;\n    loan-payments/prod)             echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-prod-package.json\" ;;\n    loan-payments_table_test/test)  echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json\" ;;\n    loan-payments_table_test/prod)  echo \"loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json\" ;;\n    *) return 1 ;;\n  esac\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# Every selected sub-project runs even after one fails, so a single run reports every failure.\nfail=0\nSUITE=\"\"\nfor sub in $SUBPROJECTS; do\n  selected \"$sub\" || continue\n  SUITE=\"$sub\"\n  args=\"$(suite \"$sub\" \"$ENV\")\" || {\n    echo \"no '$ENV' configs declared for sub-project '$sub'\" >&2\n    fail=1\n    continue\n  }\n  # shellcheck disable=SC2086  # the declaration is a space-separated argument list, on purpose\n  sqrl \"$VERB\" $args -b \"$sub\" || fail=1\ndone\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT (optional): teardown \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# [ -n \"$LIST\" ] || ./scripts/teardown.sh\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nexit $fail\n=== shared package ===\n{\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"enabled-engines\": [\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"vertx\"],\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"loan-payments.sqrl\",\n    \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": {\n        \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json\",\n        \"config\": {\n          \"is_batch\": false\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"api\": {\n      \"v1\": {\n        \"schema\": \"loan-payments-api/schema.v1.graphqls\",\n        \"operations\": [\"loan-payments-api/operations.v1.graphql\"]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\",\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n          \"enabled\": true,\n          \"defaultThresholdMs\": 200\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"compiler\": {\n    \"predicate-pushdown-rules\": \"LIMITED_RULES\",\n    \"api\": {\n      \"protocols\": [\"GRAPHQL\", \"REST\", \"MCP\"],\n      \"endpoints\": \"FULL\",\n      \"add-prefix\": true,\n      \"max-result-depth\": 2,\n      \"default-limit\": 20\n    }\n  }\n}\n=== prod package ===\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"prod\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"${AUTH_AUDIENCE}\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"${JWT_SECRET}\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n=== api test package ===\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"0 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"use-inferred-schema\": false,\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/loan-payments/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"loan-payments-api/tests/\"\n  }\n}\n=== table test package ===\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"0 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"use-inferred-schema\": true,\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/loan-payments_table_test/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"no-graphql-tests/\",\n    \"headers\": {\n      \"Authorization\": \"Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJsb2FuLXBheW1lbnRzLXRlc3QtaXNzdWVyIiwiYXVkIjpbImxvYW4tcGF5bWVudHMtdGVzdC1hdWRpZW5jZSJdLCJleHAiOjk5OTk5OTk5OTksImN1c3RvbWVyX2lkIjoiQ1VTVC0wNjAiLCJpYXQiOjE3OTU1MTEwMjZ9.PWHFR9DBUyp1W3WC7rQHPrNwmYiM20NDgc-TBjwd3Wc\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n=== README ===\n# Loan Payments\n\nA customer-facing data application for **personal loans**. It reads the shared finance\n[`data-catalog`](../data-catalog) sources `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment`, builds a payment\nhistory and a per-loan payment summary, and serves them through a JWT-authorized GraphQL, REST and\nMCP API. Each customer sees only the loans of the `customer_id` in their JWT.\n\nThe sub-project is `loan-payments`. The table tests run as a second sub-project,\n`loan-payments_table_test`.\n\n## Motivation and Requirements\n\nPersonal-loan customers sign in through the bank SSO and need to look up their own loans:\n\n- `LoanPayments`: one row per payment of a personal loan, with the customer and purpose of the\n  loan. It keeps payments in every status, so a customer still sees a returned payment.\n- `LoanPaymentSummary`: one row per personal loan, with payment counts, payment totals and the last\n  payment date. Only `APPLIED` payments contribute to the totals; a loan without payments still\n  appears with zero counts and a null last payment date.\n- Three lookups, all filtered by the JWT claim `customer_id`: a loan's summary, a loan's payments,\n  and every personal loan of the signed-in customer with its summary.\n\nAuto loans, mortgages and credit cards are out of scope, as are writes: the API has no mutation.\nThe pipeline manages no data of its own \u2014 it reuses the catalog connectors and its test data.\n\n## Data Sources\n\nBoth sources come from the shared `data-catalog` module, declared under `script.include` in\n`loan-payments-shared-package.json` and imported in `loan-payments.sqrl`. The project never copies or\nredeclares them.\n\n| Source | Test (`environment: test`) | Production (`environment: prod`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Personal_Loan` | `testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl` | Kafka topic `consumer_loans.personal_loan` |\n| `Loan_Payment` | `testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl` | Kafka topic `consumer_loans.loan_payment` |\n\nBoth are CDC streams with `ingested_at` watermarks (10 s in test, 30 s in prod). The project uses\nthe catalog watermarks as they are. The catalog's other consumer-loan tables \u2014 `Auto_Loan`,\n`Loan_Application` and `Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event` \u2014 are intentionally out of scope: the\nproject neither reads nor tests them.\n\n## Architecture and Implementation Choices\n\n| Table | What it does | In the API |\n|---|---|---|\n| `_LatestPersonalLoan` | latest version of each `Personal_Loan` by `source_updated_at`, key `loan_id`, all statuses | no |\n| `_LatestLoanPayment` \u2192 `_LatestPersonalLoanPayment` | latest version of each `Loan_Payment` by `source_updated_at`, key `payment_id`, filtered to `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` | no |\n| `LoanPaymentsSource` | personal payments inner-joined to their loan record, enriched with `customer_id` and `loan_purpose` | as the `LoanPayments` lookup |\n| `_AppliedPaymentAgg` / `_ReturnedPaymentAgg` | applied and returned payment counts and sums per loan | no |\n| `LoanPaymentSummarySource` | every latest personal loan left-joined to both aggregates | as the `LoanPaymentSummary` lookup |\n| `CustomerLoansSource` | the summary plus the loan details the client shows | as the `CustomerLoans` lookup |\n| `LoanPayments`, `LoanPaymentSummary`, `CustomerLoans` | claim-injected table functions over the sources above | yes |\n\n- **CDC dedup and no windows.** `DISTINCT ... ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC` keeps the latest\n  version per key, and the summary is an unwindowed, unbounded re-aggregation. A late or changed\n  payment updates the API within the freshness target (about one minute) instead of waiting for a\n  window boundary. The Flink state is bounded by key cardinality, not by time: the CDC dedup keeps\n  one state entry per loan and per payment, and the sources retain all payments, so state grows with\n  the number of loans and payments rather than with the stream length. At the assumed production\n  workload (up to 1,000,000 personal loans and 50,000 payments per day) the retained keys are large\n  but finite, and the \"no expiry\" retention requirement means state must grow with the retained\n  data rather than with the event rate. Windows would contradict the latest-status requirement,\n  which is why none are used. The state tables instead carry a derived event timestamp `_event_time`\n  (the greatest `ingested_at` of the rows that produced the state), so they keep valid event-time\n  semantics without a window.\n- **Loans without payments.** `LoanPaymentSummarySource` is anchored on the latest personal loans\n  and left-joins the applied-payment aggregate, so `PL-011` and the other payment-free loans appear\n  with zero counts and sums and a null `last_payment_date`. Grouping only the payments would omit\n  them.\n- **Flink + Postgres + Vert.x, no Kafka engine.** The dedup, enrichment and aggregation run in\n  Flink; the three lookups run in Postgres over indexed tables; Vert.x serves the API. There is no\n  mutation or subscription, so the `kafka` engine is not enabled \u2014 the catalog's Kafka sources are\n  ordinary external Flink connectors.\n- **Custom GraphQL schema.** `loan-payments-api/schema.v1.graphqls` fixes the exposed type and query\n  names, and `loan-payments-api/operations.v1.graphql` adds REST URIs and MCP tools. The claim\n  argument never appears as a caller argument.\n\n## API\n\n| Query | Arguments | Returns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)` | `loan_id` | one loan's summary, or `null` when the loan is not the caller's |\n| `LoanPayments(loan_id, limit, offset)` | `loan_id`, paging | the loan's payments, newest due date first |\n| `CustomerLoans(limit, offset)` | paging | every personal loan of the caller, each with its summary |\n\n`customer_id` is **not** an argument. It is read from the JWT and every query filters on it, so a\nrequest for another customer's loan returns no rows. GraphQL, REST and MCP are all exposed. Explicit\noperations: `LoanSummaryByLoan`, `LoanPaymentsByLoan`, `MyLoans`.\n\nThe API leaves out `loan_number` (`@npi @mask`), the account ids, the credit score and the DTI\nratio. Amounts stay in cents (`BIGINT`).\n\n## Authorization\n\nEvery query needs a valid JWT with the top-level claim `customer_id`; a request without a valid\ntoken is rejected (`JWT auth failed` / `Unauthorized`). The claim is mapped to a required function\nargument with `METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id'`.\n\nThe auth provider is configured under `engines.vertx.config.auth` in each test and prod overlay:\n\n- **test**: issuer `loan-payments-test-issuer`, audience `loan-payments-test-audience`, inline HS256\n  key from the fixed secret `dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string` (base64url without padding:\n  `ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw`).\n- **prod**: issuer `${AUTH_ISSUER}`, audience `${AUTH_AUDIENCE}`, key `${JWT_SECRET}` (base64url).\n\nMint a test token for any customer id:\n\n```bash\njwt encode --secret 'dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string' --alg HS256 \\\n  '{\"iss\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\", \"aud\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"], \"exp\": 9999999999, \"customer_id\": \"CUST-060\"}'\n```\n\n## How FR-8 Is Covered\n\nFR-8 says that when a payment changes status (for example `APPLIED` to `RETURNED`), the summary uses\nonly the latest status. It is covered **by construction**, not by a snapshot: `_LatestLoanPayment`\ndeduplicates on `payment_id` by `source_updated_at`, so only one version of each payment reaches\n`LoanPaymentsSource`; the unbounded aggregation then reads that single latest version. A changed\npayment therefore replaces its contribution to the counts and sums. No catalog payment changes\nstatus in the test data, and the scope forbids adding synthetic source data, so there is no\nsnapshot for it.\n\n## Data Quality and Observability\n\nThis project adds **no** pipeline-local `DQ_` or `OB_` tables:\n\n- The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from a `PERSONAL` payment to\n  `Personal_Loan`: a payment whose loan record never arrives would vanish. The catalog already\n  asserts referential integrity (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` in\n  `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`), so no capture is warranted.\n- The two sources are not independent senders, so ingestion counters add no signal; the catalog's\n  root-ontology snapshot queries already surface content changes.\n- The auto-loan exclusion is a required filter, not an anomaly, so it is not monitored.\n\nExisting coverage is reused from the catalog's lending-ontology assertions and root-ontology\nsnapshot queries.\n\n## Tests\n\nThe tests read the catalog test data through the catalog test connectors; the project adds no\nsource data. API tests and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses\none schema. The three public functions (`LoanPaymentSummary`, `LoanPayments`, `CustomerLoans`) are\nexercised through the custom schema by the API tests below, so their claim filter and ordering are\ncovered even though a `/*+test*/` table cannot inject the JWT claim:\n\n| Sub-project | Test package | Schema | Runs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `loan-payments` | `loan-payments-api-test-package.json` | custom (`use-inferred-schema: false`) | the API tests in `loan-payments-api/tests/` |\n| `loan-payments_table_test` | `loan-payments-table-test-package.json` | inferred (`use-inferred-schema: true`) | the `/*+test*/` tables in `loan-payments.sqrl` |\n\nAPI tests (each `X.graphql` with an optional `X.properties` token override):\n\n| Test | Token | Checks |\n|---|---|---|\n| `payment-returned` | `CUST-439` | `LoanPayments(\"PL-115\")` includes `LPMT-046` with status `RETURNED` and reason `R01` |\n| `loan-summary-returned` | `CUST-439` | `PL-115`: `returned_payment_count = 1`, `total_paid_cents = 635360` (the returned 57,760 is excluded) |\n| `loan-late-fees` | `CUST-061` | `PL-035`: `late_fees_paid_cents = 7500` |\n| `zero-payment-loan` | `CUST-066` | `PL-011`: zero counts and sums, null `last_payment_date` |\n| `customer-loans` | `CUST-060` | the four loans `PL-006`, `PL-007`, `PL-022`, `PL-033`, each with its summary |\n| `wrong-customer-summary` | `CUST-060` | `LoanPaymentSummary(\"PL-115\")` returns `null` |\n| `wrong-customer-payments` | `CUST-060` | `LoanPayments(\"PL-115\")` returns no rows |\n| `missing-token` | none | the request is rejected with an authorization error |\n\nTable tests:\n\n| Test | Checks |\n|---|---|\n| `LoanPaymentsCountTest` | 1,010 personal payment rows over 135 loans |\n| `AutoLoanLeakTest` (`no_rows`) | no `AL-` loan id leaked in |\n| `DuplicateLoanPaymentsTest` (`no_rows`) | one row per `payment_id` |\n| `DuplicateLoanSummaryTest` (`no_rows`) | one row per `loan_id` |\n| `ZeroPaymentSummaryTest` | a payment-free loan has zero counts/sums and a null last payment date |\n| `CustomerLoansSourceTest` | the four-loan customer `CUST-060` appears with one row per loan and its summary |\n\nExpected results are in `snapshots/loan-payments/` and `snapshots/loan-payments_table_test/`.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\nloan-payments/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments.sqrl                        # processing, lookups and table tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-shared-package.json         # base config: engines, catalog include, API\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-api-test-package.json       # custom-schema API tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-table-test-package.json     # inferred-schema table tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-prod-package.json           # production auth\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-api/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                    # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql                 # REST/MCP operations\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                                # API test queries and tokens\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments/                        # API snapshots\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 loan-payments_table_test/             # table snapshots\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n\n## Commands\n\nThe `./run-tests.sh` commands run from the project folder, which the `cd` line enters from the root\nof the repository.\n\n```bash\ncd loan-payments                             # the project folder, from the repository root\n./run-tests.sh                               # run the API tests and the table tests\n./run-tests.sh loan-payments                 # run only the API tests\n./run-tests.sh loan-payments_table_test      # run only the table tests\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod loan-payments   # compile the production pipeline\n```\n\nThe `docker run` commands make the same DataSQRL calls and work from any folder inside the git\nrepository.\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  compile -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-prod-package.json -b loan-payments\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json -b loan-payments\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json -b loan-payments_table_test\n```\n\n`-b <name>` writes the output to `build/<name>`, and it is optional.\n\nThe project has no `dev` environment; the `test` environment reads the catalog's filesystem test data\nand can be used for a local run:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -it --rm -p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081 -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  run -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json\n```\n\nWhile the pipeline runs: the Flink WebUI is at http://localhost:8081/, the GraphQL API and GraphiQL\nat http://localhost:8888/v1/graphiql/, the REST Swagger UI at http://localhost:8888/v1/swagger-ui,\nthe OpenAPI JSON at http://localhost:8888/v1/openapi, and the MCP API at\n`http://localhost:8888/v1/mcp/`. Every request needs a token from the [Authorization](#authorization)\nsection.\n\nA production deployment needs `AUTH_ISSUER`, `AUTH_AUDIENCE` and `JWT_SECRET`, and the catalog\nrequires `LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS`.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 13, "timestamp": 1795531315606}]}
