{"request_uuid": "caacdd4d-ad99-478a-ba94-72fe26930973", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T14:42:03.515974+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "patch", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20e01b88a787386ffbe5d", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Vert.x Engine Configuration\n\n| Key        | Type       | Default   | Notes                                                 |\n|------------|------------|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------|\n| `authKind` | **array**  | `[]`      | Not read by the server; `auth` needs no `authKind`    |\n| `config`   | **object** | see below | Vert.x-specific configuration including auth settings |\n\n## Server Configuration Overrides\n\nThe compiler generates the full server configuration from a built-in template and deep-merges `engines.vertx.config` over it, so any server setting can be overridden, not just authentication. Nested objects are merged key by key, while arrays replace the default array. Commonly overridden settings:\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|-----|-------|\n| `httpServerOptions.port` | HTTP port (default `8888`). Keep `8888` for the `test` command, whose test runner connects to that port |\n| `corsHandlerOptions` | CORS policy. Defaults allow all origins; restrict `allowedOrigin`/`allowedOrigins` in production |\n| `poolOptions.maxSize` | PostgreSQL connection pool size |\n| `poolOptions.maxLifetime` | Seconds before a pooled PostgreSQL connection is closed and replaced (default `600`), so connections rebalance across replicas; `0` keeps them forever |\n| `pgConnectOptions.cachePreparedStatements` | Cache prepared statements per connection so each query is planned once (default `true`) |\n| `servletConfig` | Endpoint paths (`graphQLEndpoint`, `restEndpoint`, `mcpEndpoint`, `graphiQLEndpoint`), prefixed with the API version, e.g. `/v1/graphql` |\n| `publicGraphQLEndpointEnabled` | Set to `false` to disable the public GraphQL endpoint; REST and MCP endpoints keep working |\n| `onlyConfiguredGraphQLOperations` | Set to `true` to only accept the predefined API operations instead of arbitrary GraphQL queries |\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"corsHandlerOptions\": {\n          \"allowedOrigin\": \"https://app.example.com\",\n          \"allowCredentials\": true\n        },\n        \"poolOptions\": {\n          \"maxSize\": 16\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor all settings and their effective values, compile the project and read the generated `deploy/plan/vertx-config.json` under the build directory (`build/` normally or `build/<sub-project>/` when compiling with `-b`). It contains the full server configuration with the overrides from `engines.vertx.config` applied.\n\n## Slow-Query Logging\n\nEnable slow-query logging in every project that enables `vertx`. The server then logs each API request that takes longer than its threshold or that fails. A request's threshold is the lowest threshold among the fields it runs. A slow query then appears in the logs, not only as a client timeout.\n\nPut the block in the base config, so every deployed environment has it:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n          \"enabled\": true,\n          \"defaultThresholdMs\": 2000\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig`:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the logging on |\n| `defaultThresholdMs` | `60000` | The threshold for fields without an entry in `thresholds` |\n| `thresholds` | `{}` | A threshold for requests that run this field, keyed `Type.field`, e.g. `{\"Query.OrdersByCustomer\": 5000}` or `{\"Customers.orders\": 5000}`. A request uses the lowest threshold among the fields it runs |\n| `alwaysLogErrors` | `true` | Also logs failed requests, such as a timeout while waiting for a database connection |\n| `maxLoggedTracesPerMinute` | `60` | Limits the log volume during an incident |\n| `maxFieldTraces` | `50` | Maximum number of query timings in one log line |\n\n### Choosing the threshold\n\nMake an educated guess for each threshold from how the API is used. The values in the example are a starting point, not a default to copy.\n\n- If the requirements state a latency target for the API, use that target. Otherwise, start from the consumer of the API: 1000 to 2000 ms for user interfaces and dashboards, 2000 to 5000 ms for service-to-service calls.\n- Look for heavy queries, such as a large aggregate. If their latency is much higher than that of the other queries, give each one its own entry in `thresholds`, keyed by its field (`Query.<Field>` for a table or table function, `<Type>.<field>` for a relationship). As a request uses the lowest threshold among its fields, the entry applies only when a request runs that field alone. Any other field in the same request, including a nested relationship field without its own entry, brings the lower threshold back.\n\n- Keep every threshold well below the client request timeout. The server writes the log line after a request ends, and it does not stop the request. A threshold below the timeout logs the requests that are slow but still succeed.\n\n## Large-Result Logging\n\nThe server logs a warning for each database query that returns many rows, before it converts the rows to JSON. Converting a very large result blocks the server, so the warning is written even when the pod is killed afterwards. The warning names the GraphQL field path, the row count, the elapsed time and the SQL (cut after 2000 characters), and never the parameter values. The elapsed time counts from issuing the query to receiving its rows, including any wait for a pool connection, so it is not the database time. Also when this warning is off, the server logs an error for a failed query, with its field path and SQL, and for rows it cannot convert to JSON, with the field path.\n\nThis warning is off by default and only covers large results. Slow requests are logged by [Slow-Query Logging](#slow-query-logging), which has per-field thresholds.\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.jdbcStatementLogConfig.largeResults`. `jdbcStatementLogConfig` holds the logs about individual database queries, and each has its own `enabled` flag:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the warning on |\n| `rowCountThreshold` | `10000` | Warns when a query returns at least this many rows. `0` or less turns the warning off |\n\n## Authentication\n\nConfigure `auth` with the list of trusted token issuers. Each issuer carries its own audiences and signing keys, and a\ntoken is accepted only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature all match the same issuer.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://login.example.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://app.example.com\", \"https://admin.example.com\"]\n            },\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://<tenant>.us.auth0.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://api.example.com/\"]\n            }\n          ],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Key                       | Required | Default                          | Meaning                                                                          |\n|---------------------------|----------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `issuers`                 | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty array of trusted issuers                                               |\n| `leeway`                  | No       | `60`                             | Clock skew in seconds; resolution order is `issuers[].leeway`, `leeway`, 60      |\n| `algorithms`              | No       | no restriction                   | Allowed signature algorithms; an issuer's own `algorithms` overrides it          |\n| `resource`                | No       | derived from the request         | Public MCP endpoint URL for RFC 9728 metadata                                    |\n| `scopesSupported`         | No       | `[\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"]` | Scopes advertised to MCP clients                                                 |\n| `issuers[].issuer`        | Yes      |                                  | Exact `iss` value, including a trailing slash; unique                            |\n| `issuers[].audiences`     | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty; the token's `aud` must contain one of them                            |\n| `issuers[].keys`          | No       |                                  | Inline JWKs (RFC 7517); cannot rotate, so prefer discovery                       |\n| `issuers[].jwksUri`       | No       |                                  | JWK Set URL; set at most one of `keys` and `jwksUri`                             |\n| `issuers[].leeway`        | No       | `auth.leeway`                    | Per-issuer leeway; negative values are rejected                                  |\n| `issuers[].algorithms`    | No       | `auth.algorithms`                | Per-issuer allowed algorithms                                                    |\n\nWith neither `keys` nor `jwksUri`, keys come from OIDC discovery at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so\n`issuer` must then be an absolute http(s) URL. Keys are fetched once at startup; the server fails to start if they cannot\nbe fetched, and must be restarted after a key rotation. Each key verifies tokens signed with its own `alg`, or with the\ndefault for its type (`RS256` for `RSA`, `ES256` for `EC`, `EdDSA` for `OKP`, `HS256` for `oct`); tokens signed with\nany other algorithm are rejected. Set `algorithms` to restrict this, or to use keys without `alg` for other algorithms:\na key whose `alg` is not listed is ignored, and a key without `alg` is used with each listed algorithm of its type (for\nexample `\"algorithms\": [\"PS256\"]` for an RSA key without `alg` that signs `PS256`). Compilation fails if none of an issuer's inline\n`keys` can verify signatures, because it would accept unsigned tokens; for keys from a `jwksUri` or discovery, the server\ndoes not start. Unknown keys, duplicate issuers, unsupported algorithms and the other invalid\nshapes are compile errors.\n\nThe MCP discovery endpoint `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` advertises the issuers without inline `keys` whose\n`issuer` is a URL as `authorization_servers`. Issuers with inline `keys` sign their own tokens and are not advertised;\nwhen no issuer is advertised, the endpoint is not served and MCP `401` responses carry no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n\nFor a shared secret (tests), use inline `\"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"<base64url secret>\" }]`; the `/design-api` skill's\n[authorization.md](../design-api/authorization.md#generating-tokens) shows how to encode it.\n\nString values can reference environment variables, e.g. `\"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\"`. They are resolved when the\nserver starts.\n\nPackage layers merge objects key by key but replace arrays whole: a later layer can patch `auth.leeway` alone, but a\nlayer that sets `auth.issuers` must list every issuer of its environment. `auth` cannot be combined with `jwtAuth` or\n`oauthConfig`, even across layers; set the old block to `null` in the layer that introduces `auth`.\n\n## JWT Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`jwtAuth` is deprecated; use `auth`. Its field names map to Vert.x Java POJOs and are case-sensitive. Compilation fails if it has no key that can verify signatures (for example with no `pubSecKeys`, `jwks` or `keyStore`). If none of the keys `oauthConfig` discovers can verify signatures, the server does not start.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"jwtAuth\": {\n          \"pubSecKeys\": [{\n            \"algorithm\": \"HS256\",\n            \"buffer\": \"<base64-encoded signer secret>\"\n          }],\n          \"jwtOptions\": {\n            \"issuer\": \"https://issuer.example\",\n            \"audience\": [\"my-api\"],\n            \"expiresInSeconds\": 3600,\n            \"leeway\": 30\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor asymmetric algorithms such as `ES256`, `buffer` must contain the appropriately formatted public key (normally PEM).\n\n## OAuth 2.0 Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`oauthConfig` is deprecated because it checks neither `iss` nor `aud`; use `auth`. OAuth enables MCP clients to discover the authorization server at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"oauthConfig\": {\n          \"oauth2Options\": {\n            \"site\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\",\n            \"clientId\": \"my-client-id\"\n          },\n          \"authorizationServerUrl\": \"${AUTH_EXTERNAL_URL}\",\n          \"scopesSupported\": [\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`oauth2Options.site` is required. `clientId` defaults to `datasqrl-mcp`; `authorizationServerUrl`, `scopesSupported`, and `resource` are optional. Both deprecated blocks can be set together, but then a token that either one accepts is accepted; use one `auth` block that lists every issuer with its audiences instead.\n\n`resource` is the public URL of the MCP endpoint. Without it, the server derives the discovery `resource` and the `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata=\"...\"` URL of `401` responses from each request (`X-Forwarded-Proto` or the request scheme, plus `Host`). With it, the discovery metadata returns it unchanged, and `resource_metadata` points to `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` on its origin. Set it behind a proxy that terminates TLS, so MCP clients always receive `https` URLs.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795531323515}}
{"request_uuid": "caacdd4d-ad99-478a-ba94-72fe26930973", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T14:42:03.516412+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "patch", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_end", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me read the vertx.md for auth configuration and understand `engines.vertx.config.auth`. But the requirement gives exact content, so I don't need to design it, just copy. Still, per skill MUST for configure-sqrl (triggered by editing package.json and run-tests.sh? run-tests.sh is not package.json but config-ish). The task says invoke /configure-sqrl for *-package.json. I'll invoke it (already read). \n\nNow let me consider the run-tests.sh dev environment. Requirement: declare `dev` in run-tests.sh so `./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev` compiles the loan-payments sub-project with shared + dev. Given the suite loop iterates all SUBPROJECTS with no selection, we need `loan-payments_table_test/dev` too or it errors. But the requirement only asks for the loan-payments overlay. Hmm.\n\nLet me re-read the templates: \"An unknown sub-project name, or an environment this project does not declare, is an error\". The ENVS declaration lists environments. For each selected sub-project, the suite must have a matching entry. If `dev` only covers loan-payments, then `./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev` without selecting sub-project would attempt loan-payments_table_test and fail.\n\nOptions:\n1. Add `loan-payments/dev` and `loan-payments_table_test/dev` suite entries. For table_test/dev, what configs? Could be `loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-dev-package.json` too? But then it's identical to loan-payments; `-b loan-payments_table_test`. That would read environment test. That's plausible \u2014 but the table test needs test-runner settings to actually test. For `--compile` it doesn't matter, but for `--test --env dev` it would lack test-runner. Actually the dev overlay is for running locally, not testing. Hmm.\n\nActually wait \u2014 maybe simplest interpretation: `./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev` is meant to be run and it compiles loan-payments. Since table_test has no dev config, the loop reports error and exit 1. That would contradict \"compiles ...\". But maybe the expectation is that the harness runs `./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev` and expects exit 0. Let me think about what's the intended reference behavior.\n\nLet me look at the reference repo perhaps available in git history? The task says content copied from `finance-demo/loan-payments/loan-payments-dev-package.json` in another checkout. Not here. But maybe the git repo has it in history. Let me check git log / branches.\n\nActually, let's think about the likely intended run-tests.sh change. The suite table covers every pair SUBPROJECTS x ENVS \"that this project actually has\". The requirement says \"Declare `dev` as an environment in run-tests.sh so `./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev` compiles the loan-payments sub-project\". This strongly implies adding `dev` to ENVS and adding a `loan-payments/dev` suite line. For `loan-payments_table_test/dev`, we have a choice. To make the bare `--env dev` command succeed, we need a suite entry too. \n\nMaybe map `loan-payments_table_test/dev` to `loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json`? That's not the dev overlay though. Or to `loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-dev-package.json`? The latter reads test data, which is fine for local dev, but no test-runner. Since dev is for local running (not tests), table_test/dev doesn't really make sense.\n\nWait \u2014 maybe the run-tests.sh loop should be adjusted so that when no subproject is selected, it only runs subprojects that have a config for the env? No, the template says that's explicitly forbidden (error not silent no-op).\n\nAlternatively, the ENVS declaration might be per-subproject? No, single global.\n\nLet me consider: maybe the intended change is to set SUBPROJECTS handling such that a dev env only applies to loan-payments. But the framework requires every pair.\n\nHmm, let me look at the actual repo git to see if there's the original/reference version of run-tests.sh with dev env, perhaps in git history. Let me check.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20e01b7b17b10adc43849", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/loan-payments && git log --oneline -10 2>/dev/null; echo \"---branches---\"; git branch -a 2>/dev/null; echo \"---status---\"; git status --short 2>/dev/null | head; echo \"---search dev package in git---\"; git log --all --oneline -- '*dev-package.json' 2>/dev/null | head"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20e01b88a787386ffbe5d", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/vertx.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 7230, "output": 1104, "cacheRead": 23808, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 937, "totalTokens": 32142, "cost": {"input": 0.002169, "output": 0.0013248, "cacheRead": 0.000142848, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.003636648}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795531318598, "responseId": "14ad62854c5a44919c45a2cb0b9f0573-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 4904, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, "toolResults": [{"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20e01b7b17b10adc43849", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---branches---\n---status---\n---search dev package in git---\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 10, "timestamp": 1795531323515}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20e01b88a787386ffbe5d", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Vert.x Engine Configuration\n\n| Key        | Type       | Default   | Notes                                                 |\n|------------|------------|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------|\n| `authKind` | **array**  | `[]`      | Not read by the server; `auth` needs no `authKind`    |\n| `config`   | **object** | see below | Vert.x-specific configuration including auth settings |\n\n## Server Configuration Overrides\n\nThe compiler generates the full server configuration from a built-in template and deep-merges `engines.vertx.config` over it, so any server setting can be overridden, not just authentication. Nested objects are merged key by key, while arrays replace the default array. Commonly overridden settings:\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|-----|-------|\n| `httpServerOptions.port` | HTTP port (default `8888`). Keep `8888` for the `test` command, whose test runner connects to that port |\n| `corsHandlerOptions` | CORS policy. Defaults allow all origins; restrict `allowedOrigin`/`allowedOrigins` in production |\n| `poolOptions.maxSize` | PostgreSQL connection pool size |\n| `poolOptions.maxLifetime` | Seconds before a pooled PostgreSQL connection is closed and replaced (default `600`), so connections rebalance across replicas; `0` keeps them forever |\n| `pgConnectOptions.cachePreparedStatements` | Cache prepared statements per connection so each query is planned once (default `true`) |\n| `servletConfig` | Endpoint paths (`graphQLEndpoint`, `restEndpoint`, `mcpEndpoint`, `graphiQLEndpoint`), prefixed with the API version, e.g. `/v1/graphql` |\n| `publicGraphQLEndpointEnabled` | Set to `false` to disable the public GraphQL endpoint; REST and MCP endpoints keep working |\n| `onlyConfiguredGraphQLOperations` | Set to `true` to only accept the predefined API operations instead of arbitrary GraphQL queries |\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"corsHandlerOptions\": {\n          \"allowedOrigin\": \"https://app.example.com\",\n          \"allowCredentials\": true\n        },\n        \"poolOptions\": {\n          \"maxSize\": 16\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor all settings and their effective values, compile the project and read the generated `deploy/plan/vertx-config.json` under the build directory (`build/` normally or `build/<sub-project>/` when compiling with `-b`). It contains the full server configuration with the overrides from `engines.vertx.config` applied.\n\n## Slow-Query Logging\n\nEnable slow-query logging in every project that enables `vertx`. The server then logs each API request that takes longer than its threshold or that fails. A request's threshold is the lowest threshold among the fields it runs. A slow query then appears in the logs, not only as a client timeout.\n\nPut the block in the base config, so every deployed environment has it:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n          \"enabled\": true,\n          \"defaultThresholdMs\": 2000\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig`:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the logging on |\n| `defaultThresholdMs` | `60000` | The threshold for fields without an entry in `thresholds` |\n| `thresholds` | `{}` | A threshold for requests that run this field, keyed `Type.field`, e.g. `{\"Query.OrdersByCustomer\": 5000}` or `{\"Customers.orders\": 5000}`. A request uses the lowest threshold among the fields it runs |\n| `alwaysLogErrors` | `true` | Also logs failed requests, such as a timeout while waiting for a database connection |\n| `maxLoggedTracesPerMinute` | `60` | Limits the log volume during an incident |\n| `maxFieldTraces` | `50` | Maximum number of query timings in one log line |\n\n### Choosing the threshold\n\nMake an educated guess for each threshold from how the API is used. The values in the example are a starting point, not a default to copy.\n\n- If the requirements state a latency target for the API, use that target. Otherwise, start from the consumer of the API: 1000 to 2000 ms for user interfaces and dashboards, 2000 to 5000 ms for service-to-service calls.\n- Look for heavy queries, such as a large aggregate. If their latency is much higher than that of the other queries, give each one its own entry in `thresholds`, keyed by its field (`Query.<Field>` for a table or table function, `<Type>.<field>` for a relationship). As a request uses the lowest threshold among its fields, the entry applies only when a request runs that field alone. Any other field in the same request, including a nested relationship field without its own entry, brings the lower threshold back.\n\n- Keep every threshold well below the client request timeout. The server writes the log line after a request ends, and it does not stop the request. A threshold below the timeout logs the requests that are slow but still succeed.\n\n## Large-Result Logging\n\nThe server logs a warning for each database query that returns many rows, before it converts the rows to JSON. Converting a very large result blocks the server, so the warning is written even when the pod is killed afterwards. The warning names the GraphQL field path, the row count, the elapsed time and the SQL (cut after 2000 characters), and never the parameter values. The elapsed time counts from issuing the query to receiving its rows, including any wait for a pool connection, so it is not the database time. Also when this warning is off, the server logs an error for a failed query, with its field path and SQL, and for rows it cannot convert to JSON, with the field path.\n\nThis warning is off by default and only covers large results. Slow requests are logged by [Slow-Query Logging](#slow-query-logging), which has per-field thresholds.\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.jdbcStatementLogConfig.largeResults`. `jdbcStatementLogConfig` holds the logs about individual database queries, and each has its own `enabled` flag:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the warning on |\n| `rowCountThreshold` | `10000` | Warns when a query returns at least this many rows. `0` or less turns the warning off |\n\n## Authentication\n\nConfigure `auth` with the list of trusted token issuers. Each issuer carries its own audiences and signing keys, and a\ntoken is accepted only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature all match the same issuer.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://login.example.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://app.example.com\", \"https://admin.example.com\"]\n            },\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://<tenant>.us.auth0.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://api.example.com/\"]\n            }\n          ],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Key                       | Required | Default                          | Meaning                                                                          |\n|---------------------------|----------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `issuers`                 | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty array of trusted issuers                                               |\n| `leeway`                  | No       | `60`                             | Clock skew in seconds; resolution order is `issuers[].leeway`, `leeway`, 60      |\n| `algorithms`              | No       | no restriction                   | Allowed signature algorithms; an issuer's own `algorithms` overrides it          |\n| `resource`                | No       | derived from the request         | Public MCP endpoint URL for RFC 9728 metadata                                    |\n| `scopesSupported`         | No       | `[\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"]` | Scopes advertised to MCP clients                                                 |\n| `issuers[].issuer`        | Yes      |                                  | Exact `iss` value, including a trailing slash; unique                            |\n| `issuers[].audiences`     | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty; the token's `aud` must contain one of them                            |\n| `issuers[].keys`          | No       |                                  | Inline JWKs (RFC 7517); cannot rotate, so prefer discovery                       |\n| `issuers[].jwksUri`       | No       |                                  | JWK Set URL; set at most one of `keys` and `jwksUri`                             |\n| `issuers[].leeway`        | No       | `auth.leeway`                    | Per-issuer leeway; negative values are rejected                                  |\n| `issuers[].algorithms`    | No       | `auth.algorithms`                | Per-issuer allowed algorithms                                                    |\n\nWith neither `keys` nor `jwksUri`, keys come from OIDC discovery at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so\n`issuer` must then be an absolute http(s) URL. Keys are fetched once at startup; the server fails to start if they cannot\nbe fetched, and must be restarted after a key rotation. Each key verifies tokens signed with its own `alg`, or with the\ndefault for its type (`RS256` for `RSA`, `ES256` for `EC`, `EdDSA` for `OKP`, `HS256` for `oct`); tokens signed with\nany other algorithm are rejected. Set `algorithms` to restrict this, or to use keys without `alg` for other algorithms:\na key whose `alg` is not listed is ignored, and a key without `alg` is used with each listed algorithm of its type (for\nexample `\"algorithms\": [\"PS256\"]` for an RSA key without `alg` that signs `PS256`). Compilation fails if none of an issuer's inline\n`keys` can verify signatures, because it would accept unsigned tokens; for keys from a `jwksUri` or discovery, the server\ndoes not start. Unknown keys, duplicate issuers, unsupported algorithms and the other invalid\nshapes are compile errors.\n\nThe MCP discovery endpoint `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` advertises the issuers without inline `keys` whose\n`issuer` is a URL as `authorization_servers`. Issuers with inline `keys` sign their own tokens and are not advertised;\nwhen no issuer is advertised, the endpoint is not served and MCP `401` responses carry no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n\nFor a shared secret (tests), use inline `\"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"<base64url secret>\" }]`; the `/design-api` skill's\n[authorization.md](../design-api/authorization.md#generating-tokens) shows how to encode it.\n\nString values can reference environment variables, e.g. `\"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\"`. They are resolved when the\nserver starts.\n\nPackage layers merge objects key by key but replace arrays whole: a later layer can patch `auth.leeway` alone, but a\nlayer that sets `auth.issuers` must list every issuer of its environment. `auth` cannot be combined with `jwtAuth` or\n`oauthConfig`, even across layers; set the old block to `null` in the layer that introduces `auth`.\n\n## JWT Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`jwtAuth` is deprecated; use `auth`. Its field names map to Vert.x Java POJOs and are case-sensitive. Compilation fails if it has no key that can verify signatures (for example with no `pubSecKeys`, `jwks` or `keyStore`). If none of the keys `oauthConfig` discovers can verify signatures, the server does not start.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"jwtAuth\": {\n          \"pubSecKeys\": [{\n            \"algorithm\": \"HS256\",\n            \"buffer\": \"<base64-encoded signer secret>\"\n          }],\n          \"jwtOptions\": {\n            \"issuer\": \"https://issuer.example\",\n            \"audience\": [\"my-api\"],\n            \"expiresInSeconds\": 3600,\n            \"leeway\": 30\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor asymmetric algorithms such as `ES256`, `buffer` must contain the appropriately formatted public key (normally PEM).\n\n## OAuth 2.0 Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`oauthConfig` is deprecated because it checks neither `iss` nor `aud`; use `auth`. OAuth enables MCP clients to discover the authorization server at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"oauthConfig\": {\n          \"oauth2Options\": {\n            \"site\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\",\n            \"clientId\": \"my-client-id\"\n          },\n          \"authorizationServerUrl\": \"${AUTH_EXTERNAL_URL}\",\n          \"scopesSupported\": [\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`oauth2Options.site` is required. `clientId` defaults to `datasqrl-mcp`; `authorizationServerUrl`, `scopesSupported`, and `resource` are optional. Both deprecated blocks can be set together, but then a token that either one accepts is accepted; use one `auth` block that lists every issuer with its audiences instead.\n\n`resource` is the public URL of the MCP endpoint. Without it, the server derives the discovery `resource` and the `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata=\"...\"` URL of `401` responses from each request (`X-Forwarded-Proto` or the request scheme, plus `Host`). With it, the discovery metadata returns it unchanged, and `resource_metadata` points to `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` on its origin. Set it behind a proxy that terminates TLS, so MCP clients always receive `https` URLs.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795531323515}]}
